window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-1", "text": "How many instructions does this circuit contain, what is its depth, and what state does it prepare? When does the Python loop run? The loop runs at construction time, in Python, and appends three separate H instructions: count_ops reports {'h': 3} and the depth is \\(3\\). Since \\(H^2=I\\), the state is \\(H\\ket0=\\ket+\\). The quantum device never sees a loop; it receives three gates, which a compiler may simplify to one."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-2", "text": "Classify Q#, OpenQASM, and Qiskit as a standalone quantum programming language, a circuit-level description language, or a framework embedded in a host language. Which of them is a Python library? Q# is a standalone quantum programming language with its own syntax and compiler. OpenQASM is a circuit-level language for describing operations, measurements, and classical control, often used as an exchange format between tools. Qiskit is a Python SDK: a library that uses Python to construct circuit objects, which it can then compile, simulate, export, or submit. Its programs are Python programs that build quantum instructions."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-3", "text": "What happens when you ask for the inverse of a circuit that ends in a measurement, and why? Qiskit raises a CircuitError, “inverse() not implemented for measure.” A measurement maps different input states to the same output record and has no unitary inverse, so no automatic adjoint exists. Only purely unitary routines can be inverted gate by gate; a routine containing measurement or reset needs a different treatment, such as removing the measurement before inverting."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-4", "text": "Predict the statevector produced by each circuit before running it: (a) H on qubit 1 followed by cx(1, 0); (b) H on qubit 1 followed by cx(0, 1). Qiskit lists amplitudes in the order \\(00,01,10,11\\) of the labels \\(q_1q_0\\). Which circuit makes a Bell state? (a) H puts qubit 1 in \\(\\ket+\\); cx(1, 0) flips qubit 0 when qubit 1 is \\(1\\), giving \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), the same Bell state as the chapter’s circuit, because that state is symmetric under exchanging the qubits. (b) The control, qubit 0, is \\(\\ket0\\), so the CNOT does nothing: the state is \\(\\ket+\\) on qubit 1 and \\(\\ket0\\) on qubit 0, with amplitudes \\((0.707,0,0.707,0)\\), a product state. The roles of control and target, not just which gates appear, determine the result."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-5", "text": "Build \\(U\\), which applies H and then S to one qubit. (a) Predict the instructions of U.inverse(). (b) Check that composing U with its inverse gives the identity operator. (c) Why must the inverse reverse the order as well as invert each gate? (a) The inverse lists sdg, then h. (b) The composition is the identity operator, which Operator equivalence confirms. (c) \\(U=SH\\) as a matrix (H acts first), so \\(U^{-1}=H^{-1}S^{-1}=HS^\\dagger\\): the last gate applied must be undone first, exactly as when unwinding nested function calls. Inverting each gate without reversing the order would give \\(S^\\dagger H\\), which is not the inverse."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-6", "text": "A student wants to apply X to qubit 1 only if a measurement of qubit 0 gives 1, and writes a Python if statement that inspects a variable holding the measurement result. Explain why this cannot work at construction time, and write the version that records the condition as an instruction executed during the run. A Python if runs while the circuit is being built, before any shot executes, so no measured value exists to inspect. The condition must become part of the circuit: an instruction that refers to the classical bit and is evaluated by the controller during each run. In Qiskit, the if_test context adds an if_else instruction whose body applies X when the bit is 1."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-7", "text": "Export the Bell circuit to OpenQASM 3 with qiskit.qasm3.dumps. Identify which output line declares the qubits and which lines are the gates. What information would an exporter need in order to represent the circuit on a specific device, beyond this text? The output contains a header (OPENQASM 3.0 and an include of the standard gates), a declaration qubit[2] q, and the gates h q[0] and cx q[0], q[1]. This describes the logical circuit. A device implementation also needs the native gate decomposition, the mapping of logical to physical qubits, any routing, and timing or pulse details. Exporting preserves meaning, not a device-specific realization."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reasons: “If r = q refers to the same qubit, then cx(q, r) uses one qubit as control and its copy as target.” What does Qiskit do with this code, and what physical principle does the check reflect? Qiskit raises a CircuitError, “duplicate bit arguments.” Assigning r = q creates a second name for the same qubit, not a copy of its state; no-cloning forbids making an independent copy of an unknown state. A CNOT needs two different qubits, and using one wire as both control and target is meaningless. Qiskit catches this when the gate is appended; a language with linear types could reject it before the program runs."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-9", "text": "A linear type system requires every qubit handle to be used exactly once and forbids duplicating it. (a) Which of these would it reject at compile time: a function that returns the same qubit twice; cx(q, q); a function that silently drops a qubit that is entangled with others? (b) What does Qiskit check instead, and when? (c) Give one benefit and one cost of static enforcement. (a) A linear type system rejects all three: returning a qubit twice and cx(q, q) both duplicate a handle, and dropping an entangled qubit discards a resource without an explicit operation (an affine system would allow discarding but not duplication). (b) Qiskit is a Python library, so it checks at run time of the construction code: duplicate arguments raise an error when the gate is appended, while silently ignoring a qubit is allowed. (c) Static checks catch mistakes before any execution and document resource use; the cost is a stricter language in which some valid programs, such as those that measure and discard deliberately, need extra annotations."}, {"title": "Quantum Programming Languages — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#exercise-10", "text": "A one-qubit circuit applies X with global_phase \\(\\pi/2\\), so it implements \\(iX\\). (a) Uncontrolled, how does it differ from X? (b) Form its controlled version with control(1) and inspect the matrix. What happened to the phase? (c) Why must a programming framework track global phase for this reason? (a) Uncontrolled, \\(iX\\) and \\(X\\) differ by a global phase and are physically indistinguishable. (b) The controlled matrix maps the control-1 states by \\(iX\\) and leaves the control-0 states alone: the factor \\(i\\) now distinguishes the two branches, so the controlled version is CNOT followed by S on the control. (c) A routine that is correct “up to global phase” can become wrong when controlled. Qiskit stores global_phase on the circuit and includes it in control(), so routines used inside controlled operations must have their phase specified, not discarded."}, {"title": "Language Families", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 Language Families A circuit drawing can say “apply H, then controlled-X, then measure.” A programming system must also decide what the qubits refer to, when measurements occur, and which instructions the hardware can execute. Language design makes these choices explicit. 1.1.1 Imperative and functional approaches An imperative description lists actions on named registers in time order. A functional description emphasizes composition of transformations and return values. Both can describe the same Bell preparation: create two zeros, apply H to the first, and controlled-X to the second. The programming style does not change the underlying state. Functional interfaces can make composition and resource use explicit, while imperative interfaces often resemble the circuit directly. 1.1.2 Standalone languages and embedded frameworks A standalone language has its own syntax and compiler; an embedded framework uses a host language to construct quantum instructions. Q# is a quantum programming language, OpenQASM describes circuit-level operations and classical control, and Qiskit is a Python SDK. A Python loop that appends gates runs while building a circuit; it is not automatically a loop executed by the quantum controller. Problem 1.1. Does assigning other = circuit create an independent circuit? Worked solution No. Both Python names refer to the same mutable object. Use circuit.copy() when subsequent edits must be independent. Neither action copies a physical quantum state."}, {"title": "Design Principles", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Design Principles 1.2.1 Types, ownership, and no-cloning A qubit handle names a physical or logical resource. Assigning another variable to that handle does not copy its quantum state. Linear or affine type disciplines can restrict duplicated use or disposal of resources; no-cloning is a physical constraint even when a language does not enforce it statically. A robust interface also prevents accidentally using the same wire as both independent inputs to a gate. 1.2.2 Reversibility, measurement, and classical control Unitary routines can have adjoints; a routine containing measurement or reset generally cannot be inverted as a unitary. Measurement produces a classical record that may select later operations. A host-language if statement on a circuit object cannot inspect an unexecuted qubit. Classical control during execution needs an instruction that refers to an actual recorded bit. Problem 1.2. Can every quantum function have an automatic inverse? Worked solution Only operations with a specified reversible action can be inverted this way. Measurement with a discarded outcome and reset lose information in the circuit-level description."}, {"title": "Abstraction and Execution", "chapter": "Quantum Programming Languages", "url": "01-quantum-programming-languages.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Abstraction and Execution 1.3.1 Circuit languages and intermediate representations An intermediate representation preserves the operations and dependencies while a compiler changes their implementation. Logical H and CNOT gates may become native rotations and entangling gates with inserted routing. The language should separate a circuit’s mathematical meaning from its device-specific realization. Exporting a circuit does not guarantee that every destination supports its timing or control constructs. 1.3.2 Qiskit as a Python SDK The companion builds a Bell circuit in Python, then asks a simulator for its state. The function returns a description, while Statevector performs a classical calculation of that description. The two roles are separate. Predict amplitudes (1/√2,0,0,1/√2) before running it; only then use the assertion as a check. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_01.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 1.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector\n\n# A Python function builds a circuit; it does not execute a quantum device.\ndef bell():\n    circuit = QuantumCircuit(2)\n    circuit.h(0)\n    circuit.cx(0, 1)\n    return circuit\n\ncircuit = bell()\nassert Statevector.from_instruction(circuit).equiv(\n    Statevector([2**-0.5, 0, 0, 2**-0.5]))\nprint(circuit.draw(output=\"text\")) Problem 1.3. Why distinguish construction time from execution time? Worked solution The host may expand a loop into a fixed gate sequence before any quantum run. A measurement-dependent loop requires controller support and cannot be resolved during ordinary circuit construction. We will use Python to build circuits and explicit execution interfaces to run them. The next chapter constructs a reproducible local environment. Sources and further study: OpenQASM language specification ; Selinger and Valiron, quantum lambda calculus . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 1", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(1024\\) fair-coin shots, find the expected count of 1 and the standard deviations of the count and of the frequency. Would \\(540\\) ones be surprising? Expected count \\(512\\); count standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{1024/4}=16\\); frequency standard deviation \\(1/(2\\sqrt{1024})=1/64\\approx0.016\\). A count of \\(540\\) is \\(28/16\\approx1.75\\) standard deviations high: unusual but not alarming. Tests should use tolerances of several standard deviations, chosen in advance."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 2", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-2", "text": "A circuit is built with its own classical register: QuantumCircuit(1, 1), H, then measure(0, 0). The sampler result has no field named meas. Under which name are the counts stored, and why? The counts are under data.c. QuantumCircuit(1, 1) creates a default classical register named c, and the sampler’s result fields are named after the circuit’s classical registers. measure_all adds a new register named meas, which is why the chapter’s example used data.meas. Code that hard-codes the name breaks when the circuit is built differently."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 3", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-3", "text": "Which package is needed for each task: (a) sampling an ideal circuit locally with StatevectorSampler; (b) simulating with a noise model; (c) submitting a job to an IBM device? Does importing the remote client submit anything? (a) The Qiskit SDK. (b) Qiskit Aer, the separate simulation package with noise models. (c) IBM Runtime. Importing the Runtime client only loads code; submitting requires credentials, a chosen backend, and an explicit run call, which is why the course keeps such calls behind explicit options."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 4", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-4", "text": "Prepare a qubit with ry(\\(\\theta\\)) so that it reads 1 with probability \\(0.75\\). (a) Find \\(\\theta\\) using \\(P(1)=\\sin^2(\\theta/2)\\). (b) For \\(4096\\) shots, find the expected count and a five-standard-deviation tolerance on the frequency. (c) Write a test that checks the sampled frequency against this tolerance. (a) \\(\\theta/2=\\pi/3\\), so \\(\\theta=2\\pi/3\\). (b) Expected count \\(3072\\); the frequency standard deviation is \\(\\sqrt{0.1875/4096}\\approx0.0068\\), so a five-sigma tolerance is about \\(0.034\\). (c) The test below asserts that the observed frequency lies within that tolerance of \\(0.75\\); with a fixed seed it is also a reproducible regression test."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 5", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-5", "text": "The chapter’s test asserts \\(|f-0.5|<0.05\\) for \\(4096\\) fair-coin shots. (a) How many standard deviations is \\(0.05\\)? Estimate how often a correct circuit fails the test. (b) A buggy circuit has \\(P(1)=0.45\\). How often does it pass? (c) What does this say about choosing shots and tolerance together? (a) \\(0.05/0.0078\\approx6.4\\) standard deviations; a correct circuit fails with probability around \\(10^{-10}\\). (b) For \\(p=0.45\\), the observed frequency is centred on \\(0.45\\), exactly at the boundary, so the bug passes about half the time. (c) A wide tolerance avoids false alarms but misses moderate bugs. Choosing the smallest error that must be detected, and then the shots needed to resolve it at the desired confidence, gives a test that is both reliable and sensitive; checking exact probabilities with Statevector avoids sampling altogether when possible."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 6", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-6", "text": "Run a single sampler call on two circuits: H then measure, and X then measure, with \\(1000\\) shots and a fixed seed. How do you access each circuit’s counts, and what should each contain? The result is indexed in submission order: result()[0] for the H circuit and result()[1] for the X circuit, each with its own data.meas. The H counts are near \\(500/500\\); the X counts are exactly {'1': 1000}. Batching circuits in one call is efficient, but the index ties each result to its circuit, so the order must be preserved."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 7", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-7", "text": "Show what a seed does. (a) Run the fair-coin circuit twice with seed 7 and once with seed 8. Which counts agree? (b) What does a fixed seed guarantee, and what does it not guarantee? (a) The two seed-7 runs give identical counts; the seed-8 run gives different counts, still near \\(2048\\). (b) A seed makes a particular sampling run repeatable with the same software and settings, which is valuable for regression tests. It does not make the result “correct,” it does not guarantee identical counts across library versions or simulators, and it does not remove statistical uncertainty: a seeded sample is still one sample."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 8", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student increases the shots from \\(4096\\) to \\(40960\\) with the same seed and expects every count to be exactly ten times larger. Why is this wrong, and how do the frequency fluctuations change? Counts are random samples: with more shots the sampler draws a different and longer sequence, so the new counts are not a scaled copy of the old ones. What changes predictably is the precision: the frequency standard deviation shrinks by \\(\\sqrt{10}\\approx3.2\\), from about \\(0.0078\\) to \\(0.0025\\). Expect the frequency to move closer to \\(0.5\\) on average, not the counts to scale exactly."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 9", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-9", "text": "Write a reusable test helper that decides whether counts from a fair-coin circuit are consistent with probability \\(\\frac12\\), using a tolerance of \\(z\\) standard deviations of the frequency. Apply it to a seeded run. How would you choose \\(z\\) for a suite of \\(100\\) such tests? The helper below computes the tolerance \\(z/(2\\sqrt R)\\) and compares the observed frequency. With \\(z=5\\) a correct test fails with probability under \\(10^{-6}\\), so even \\(100\\) tests rarely raise a false alarm (about \\(10^{-4}\\) in total); with \\(z=2\\), about \\(5\\%\\) of correct tests would fail somewhere in every run of the suite. The number of tests, the acceptable false-alarm rate, and the smallest bug to catch together determine \\(z\\) and the shots."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Environment — Exercise 10", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#exercise-10", "text": "For deterministic properties, sampling is unnecessary. Use Statevector to obtain the exact outcome probabilities of the ry(\\(2\\pi/3\\)) circuit (after removing its final measurements), and test them against \\(0.25\\) and \\(0.75\\). When is sampling still the right test? Statevector computes the exact probabilities \\(0.25\\) and \\(0.75\\), so the test can use a tight numerical tolerance and has no statistical failures. Sampling remains the right test when the behavior being checked is statistical or involves classical processing of samples: estimator code, mid-circuit measurements with feedback, noise simulations, or the sampler interface itself. Use exact methods for the mathematics and sampling for the pipeline."}, {"title": "Python and Environments", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Python and Environments A first program should have a result we can predict without the computer. Starting from zero, H makes equal amplitudes; measurement should give a fair coin. We will separate that prediction from random fluctuations in a finite run. 2.1.1 Python through circuit examples In circuit = QuantumCircuit(1) , assignment binds a Python name to a one-qubit circuit. A method call such as circuit.h(0) appends an instruction on qubit index zero. Parentheses supply arguments; indentation groups statements in functions and loops. A dictionary such as counts maps a measured bit string to the number of times it occurred. The program below uses these features only where the experiment needs them. 2.1.2 Packages, versions, and isolated environments Create an isolated environment rather than mixing course dependencies with unrelated projects. From the repository root, run the commands below. The requirements file pins the versions tested for this edition; the full environment lock records transitive packages. Use the accompanying TESTING.md for the tested Python version and platform notes. python3 -m venv .venv-quantum\nsource .venv-quantum/bin/activate\npython -m pip install -r education/programming/requirements.txt\npython education/programming/examples/chapter_02.py Problem 2.1. Why should a fair coin not be tested by demanding exactly 2048 ones? Worked solution Even an ideal fair source fluctuates. Exact equality would reject many correct executions. Test a distribution with a justified tolerance and test the unitary separately."}, {"title": "The Qiskit Ecosystem", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 The Qiskit Ecosystem 2.2.1 SDK, Aer, and Runtime The Qiskit SDK supplies circuit construction, quantum-information objects, transpilation, and reference primitives. Aer is a separate simulation package with noise models and additional simulation methods. IBM Runtime supplies remote execution services and associated primitives. Local examples need no cloud token; importing a remote client is not the same as submitting a job. 2.2.2 Notebooks and executable scripts A script executes from top to bottom in a fresh process. A notebook can retain variables from earlier cells, making a seemingly reproducible result depend on hidden execution order. Restart and run all cells when using notebooks, and keep the tested experiment in a script. Record versions, seeds, parameters, and shots; a seed fixes a particular pseudorandom experiment only within its implementation context. Problem 2.2. What does changing the seed change? Worked solution It changes the pseudorandom shot sequence, not the ideal quantum probability. A changed probability indicates a different circuit, model, or execution setting."}, {"title": "A First Program", "chapter": "The Qiskit Environment", "url": "02-the-qiskit-environment.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 A First Program 2.3.1 Build, run, and inspect The example creates H|0〉, adds measurement with measure_all(), and passes the resulting circuit to StatevectorSampler. The primitive returns a job; result()[0] selects its first workload result. The automatically created classical register is named meas, so data.meas.get_counts() returns its counts. The simulator internally computes amplitudes, but the requested result is a sampled classical record. 2.3.2 Predictions and reproducible results For R=4096 fair-coin shots, the expected count of one is 2048 and the standard deviation of its frequency is \\(1/(2\\sqrt R)=1/128\\). The assertion allows a much wider interval than that fluctuation. It checks a plausible distribution and total shot count, not an exact random sequence. A fixed seed helps regression testing, but a single passing sample is not a proof that H is correct. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_02.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 2.\"\"\"\nimport sys\nfrom importlib.metadata import version\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\n\ncircuit = QuantumCircuit(1)\ncircuit.h(0)\ncircuit.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=7).run([circuit], shots=4096).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert sum(counts.values()) == 4096\nassert abs(counts.get(\"1\", 0) / 4096 - 0.5) < 0.05\nprint(\"Python\", sys.version.split()[0], \"Qiskit\", version(\"qiskit\"))\nprint(counts) Problem 2.3. Where are amplitudes measured in this example? Worked solution They are not physically measured. The reference simulator computes them classically to generate samples. Hardware can provide measurement outcomes, not its complete internal state vector. The next circuit has two qubits. Its correlations are simple, but its bit ordering must be made explicit. Sources and further study: IBM, SDK primitive simulation ."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-1", "text": "A three-qubit circuit applies X only to qubit 2. Predict its bit-string label and its statevector index before running it. The label is 100 and the index is \\(4\\): qubit 2 is the most significant of three bits. probabilities_dict returns {'100': 1.0}. Reading labels left to right as qubits 0, 1, 2 would wrongly predict 001."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-2", "text": "Qubit 0 is flipped to 1, and qubit 1 is left at 0. The circuit then measures qubit 0 into classical bit 1 and qubit 1 into classical bit 0. What bit string appears in the counts? Classical bit 1 receives \\(1\\) and classical bit 0 receives \\(0\\), and count strings list classical bits from highest to lowest, so every shot gives 10. The physical state is the same \\(\\ket{q_1q_0}=\\ket{01}\\) as without the swapped mapping; only the storage changed. Decoding counts requires knowing the measurement map."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-3", "text": "For the state prepared by X on qubit 0 of two qubits, predict the expectation values of the Pauli labels ZI and IZ. ZI acts with \\(Z\\) on qubit 1, which is \\(\\ket0\\): expectation \\(+1\\). IZ acts on qubit 0, which is \\(\\ket1\\): expectation \\(-1\\). Swapping the two predictions is the typical symptom of reading Pauli labels in the wrong order."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-4", "text": "A Bell-pair subcircuit (H on its qubit 0, CX from 0 to 1) should be placed on qubits 2 and 0 of a three-qubit circuit. A student writes the code below and finds that big has no instructions. (a) Why? (b) Fix it. (c) Predict the resulting state’s labels. (a) compose returns a new circuit and leaves big unchanged unless inplace=True is given; the returned circuit was discarded. (b) Assign the result (or pass inplace=True). (c) The mapping puts H on qubit 2 and a CX from qubit 2 to qubit 0, entangling those two while qubit 1 stays \\(\\ket0\\): the labels are 000 and 101, each with probability \\(\\frac12\\)."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-5", "text": "A Toffoli gate is drawn as one box. Decompose it once and count the resulting instructions. What does the count suggest about judging circuit cost from a drawing? One level of decomposition gives \\(6\\) CNOTs, \\(4\\) \\(T\\) gates, \\(3\\) \\(T^\\dagger\\) gates, and \\(2\\) Hadamards: fifteen elementary instructions for one drawn box. On fault-tolerant hardware the seven \\(T\\)-type gates dominate the cost. A compact drawing says nothing about resources; counts must be taken after decomposition, ideally after transpiling to the target gate set."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-6", "text": "Counts of only 00 and 11 do not distinguish a Bell state from a classical mixture of \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\). Write a check that does: apply H to both qubits before measuring, and compute the exact outcome probabilities for both preparations, using DensityMatrix for the mixture. After \\(H\\otimes H\\), the Bell state still gives only 00 and 11, each with probability \\(\\frac12\\), while the mixture gives all four outcomes with probability \\(\\frac14\\). Exact probabilities make the test deterministic; in an experiment, the same comparison is made with sampled counts and a tolerance. Checking a second basis is what certifies coherence."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-7", "text": "Prepare a five-qubit GHZ state \\((\\ket{00000}+\\ket{11111})/\\sqrt2\\) in two ways: a chain of CNOTs \\(0\\to1\\to2\\to3\\to4\\), and a tree in which qubit 0 controls 1 and 2, then qubit 1 controls 3 while qubit 0 controls 4. Compare their depths and verify that the states agree. The chain has depth \\(5\\) (H plus four sequential CNOTs); the tree has depth \\(4\\), because the CNOTs \\(0\\to2\\) and \\(1\\to3\\) run in the same layer. Both prepare the same state. For \\(n\\) qubits a tree needs depth about \\(1+\\lceil\\log_2n\\rceil\\), against \\(n\\) for a chain, so the same mathematical state can be much shallower, an important difference when qubits decohere while waiting."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reads count strings from left to right in the same order as the wires in the text drawing, top to bottom, and concludes that 01 means “qubit 0 is 0 and qubit 1 is 1.” Show with a one-gate example that this is backwards. Apply X to qubit 0 only: the label is 01. The rightmost character is qubit 0, which is \\(1\\); the leftmost is qubit 1, which is \\(0\\). The drawing lists qubit 0 at the top, but the bit string lists it last, following the integer convention in which qubit 0 is the least significant bit. Every analysis that maps strings back to qubits must use this rule, or reverse the order explicitly."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-9", "text": "Some textbooks write the first qubit leftmost. Use Statevector.reverse_qargs() to convert the state of X on qubit 0 into that convention, and explain when such a conversion is appropriate and when it hides a bug. In Qiskit’s convention the state is labelled 01; after reverse_qargs() it is labelled 10, matching a convention in which qubit 0 is written first. Converting is appropriate at a single, documented boundary, for example when comparing with a formula from a paper. Scattering conversions through a program, or reversing to make a failing test pass without understanding why, can mask a genuine wiring or measurement-map error."}, {"title": "Circuit Construction — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#exercise-10", "text": "A circuit has two classical registers: a (one bit) measuring qubit 0, and b (two bits) measuring qubits 1 and 2. Qubit 1 is flipped. Predict the counts stored for each register and the combined string returned by join_data(). Why is it safer to read registers by name? Register a reports 0 (qubit 0 is \\(\\ket0\\)). Register b reports 01: its bit 0 holds qubit 1 (\\(1\\)) and its bit 1 holds qubit 2 (\\(0\\)). join_data() concatenates the registers, here as 010, with b on the left because it was added after a. Reading registers by name avoids depending on concatenation order, which changes if the circuit is built differently."}, {"title": "Registers and Gates", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 Registers and Gates Prepare a Bell pair and measure it. Only equal bits should appear, but that symmetric example can conceal an ordering mistake. We will add an asymmetric test before trusting the program. 3.1.1 Quantum and classical registers Qubits carry quantum state; classical bits receive measurement records. QuantumCircuit(2) creates two quantum wires, indexed zero and one. Appending H on zero and CX(0,1) means the first is the control and the second the target. A two-qubit state here is written |q1 q0〉; the leftmost displayed ket bit is not the first Python index. 3.1.2 Gate order and circuit composition Instructions are appended in chronological order. In a matrix product, the last applied gate appears on the left. Circuit composition appends another circuit with an explicit wire mapping. A circuit box may stand for many elementary gates; decomposition reveals its contents, while transpilation additionally adapts them to a gate set and connectivity. Problem 3.1. Would counts containing only 00 and 11 prove entanglement? Worked solution No. A classical mixture of those states has the same computational-basis distribution. An XX measurement can distinguish that mixture from the ideal Bell state."}, {"title": "Measurement", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Measurement 3.2.1 Mapping qubits to classical bits Measurement maps a selected quantum wire to a selected classical bit. measure_all() adds a register and measures every qubit, whereas measure(qubit, clbit) lets you choose the mapping. Count strings describe the classical storage, so changing that mapping changes how the result should be decoded even when the physical state is unchanged. 3.2.2 Bit strings and tensor-product ordering Qiskit’s integer convention makes qubit zero the least significant bit. Applying X only to qubit zero gives |01〉 and state-vector index one. The label ZI acts on qubit one, while IZ acts on qubit zero. Reversing a drawing for visual convenience does not automatically reverse these mathematical conventions. The asymmetric check in the companion catches confusion that Bell-state counts cannot expose. Problem 3.2. What state results from X on qubit one alone? Worked solution It is |10〉, with state-vector index two. This is the opposite single-bit position from X on qubit zero."}, {"title": "Circuit Inspection", "chapter": "Circuit Construction", "url": "03-circuit-construction.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 Circuit Inspection 3.3.1 Drawing and decomposing circuits A text circuit drawing is available without plotting dependencies. Read its wires from left to right in time, but read a returned bit string according to its classical register ordering. Inspect gate counts and decompositions when a familiar-looking box hides arithmetic or multi-controlled gates. Visual compactness is not a resource bound. 3.3.2 Comparing code with predicted evolution The Bell state is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). Ideal measurements therefore never return 01 or 10. Counts fluctuate between 00 and 11. The companion checks support, then uses X on only qubit zero to verify index order. Testing a second measurement basis would additionally distinguish a coherent Bell pair from a classical mixture. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_03.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 3.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector\n\nbell = QuantumCircuit(2)\nbell.h(0)\nbell.cx(0, 1)\nbell.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=8).run([bell], shots=1024).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert set(counts) <= {\"00\", \"11\"}\n# An asymmetric check is essential: a Bell state hides reversed bit order.\nasymmetric = QuantumCircuit(2)\nasymmetric.x(0)\nassert Statevector.from_instruction(asymmetric).probabilities_dict() == {\"01\": 1.0}\nprint(bell.draw(output=\"text\"))\nprint(counts) Problem 3.3. Why include a text drawing in a runnable example? Worked solution It makes the appended gate order and wire targets inspectable. The numerical test still supplies the stronger check of the circuit’s action. With the indexing fixed, we can compare circuits directly with their mathematical states and operators. Sources and further study: IBM, bit ordering . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 1", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-1", "text": "Two qubits are prepared with H on qubit 0 only. Predict partial_trace(state, [1]) and partial_trace(state, [0]). partial_trace(state, [1]) discards qubit 1 and keeps qubit 0, giving \\(\\ket+\\bra+\\), the matrix with all entries \\(\\frac12\\). partial_trace(state, [0]) keeps qubit 1, giving \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\). For a product state each reduced state is pure; for the Bell state both would be \\(I/2\\)."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 2", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-2", "text": "Predict the Bell-state expectation values of ZZ, XX, YY, and XZ before computing them. \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\), \\(\\langle XX\\rangle=1\\), \\(\\langle YY\\rangle=-1\\), and \\(\\langle XZ\\rangle=0\\). The value of YY follows from \\(Y\\otimes Y=-(X\\otimes X)(Z\\otimes Z)\\), and both factors are \\(+1\\) on the Bell state. Expectation values of several Paulis are a compact fingerprint of a state that can be checked exactly in simulation and estimated in experiment."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 3", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-3", "text": "A dense operator on \\(n\\) qubits has \\(4^n\\) complex entries of \\(16\\) bytes each. Estimate its memory for \\(n=10\\) and \\(n=20\\). What does this imply for verifying circuits with Operator? \\(n=10\\): about \\(1.05\\times10^6\\) entries, \\(17\\) MB. \\(n=20\\): about \\(1.1\\times10^{12}\\) entries, \\(18\\) TB. Operator is an excellent check for small subcircuits, a dozen qubits or so, but not a general simulation strategy; larger tests need statevectors, structured simulators, or properties checked on samples."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 4", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-4", "text": "Replace H by ry(\\(\\pi/3\\)) in the chapter’s companion, followed by the same CNOT. Predict the reduced state of qubit 0 and the expectations of ZZ and XX, then verify them. The state is \\(\\cos\\frac\\pi6\\ket{00}+\\sin\\frac\\pi6\\ket{11}\\). Qubit 0’s reduced state is \\(\\mathrm{diag}(0.75,0.25)\\) with no off-diagonal terms. \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\), since both terms have equal bits, and \\(\\langle XX\\rangle=\\sin\\frac\\pi3\\approx0.866\\). The reduced state alone looks like a classical mixture; the \\(XX\\) correlation reveals the coherent superposition."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 5", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-5", "text": "Compute the purity \\(\\mathrm{tr}\\rho^2\\) of the Bell state and of its one-qubit reduced state using DensityMatrix.purity(). Interpret the two numbers. The two-qubit Bell state has purity \\(1\\): it is pure. Its reduced state \\(I/2\\) has purity \\(\\frac12\\), the minimum for a qubit. Purity is a convenient numerical check: a globally pure state whose parts are mixed must be entangled, and the purity of the parts measures how strongly."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 6", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-6", "text": "Define \\(H=0.5\\,ZZ-0.3\\,XI\\) as a SparsePauliOp. Predict its expectation value in the Bell state and in \\(\\ket{++}\\), and verify. Bell state: \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\) and \\(\\langle XI\\rangle=0\\), so \\(\\langle H\\rangle=0.5\\). For \\(\\ket{++}\\): \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=0\\) and \\(\\langle XI\\rangle=1\\), so \\(\\langle H\\rangle=-0.3\\). Linear combinations of Pauli strings are evaluated term by term, which is also how an experiment estimates them, one measurement setting per group of compatible terms."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 7", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-7", "text": "Verify the identity “Hadamards on the target turn CX into CZ” with Operator equivalence. Then explain why Operator.equiv, rather than exact matrix equality, is the right comparison. The circuit H on qubit 1, CX from 0 to 1, H on qubit 1 has the same operator as CZ, and Operator.equiv confirms it. equiv tests equality up to a global phase within a numerical tolerance, which is what “the same gate” means physically. Exact equality would fail on harmless phase conventions or rounding, while a relative phase error would still be caught."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 8", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student calls Operator on a circuit that contains a measurement, expecting “the matrix of the circuit.” What happens, and what should they do to inspect the unitary part? Qiskit raises a QiskitError, “Cannot apply operation with classical bits: measure.” A measurement has no unitary matrix. To inspect the unitary part, remove the final measurements (remove_final_measurements(inplace=False)) and build the Operator of what remains; mid-circuit measurements need a channel or instrument description, not a unitary."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 9", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-9", "text": "Estimate \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle\\) and \\(\\langle XX\\rangle\\) for the ry(\\(\\pi/3\\)) state from sampled counts, as an experiment would: measure directly for ZZ, and apply H to both qubits before measuring for XX. Compare with the exact values \\(1\\) and \\(0.866\\), and explain the size of the discrepancy. With \\(8192\\) shots, the ZZ estimate is exactly \\(1\\) (only 00 and 11 occur) and the XX estimate is near \\(0.866\\), within about \\(\\sqrt{(1-0.75)/8192}\\approx0.006\\). The XX measurement requires a different circuit: one setting per non-commuting observable. Exact expectations check the program; sampled estimates model what an experiment reports, with an uncertainty that must be carried along."}, {"title": "States and Operators — Exercise 10", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#exercise-10", "text": "A statevector of \\(n\\) qubits needs \\(2^n\\) amplitudes of \\(16\\) bytes. (a) Estimate the memory for \\(n=30\\) and \\(n=100\\). (b) Run a \\(100\\)-qubit GHZ circuit with Aer’s stabilizer method and check the outcomes. (c) Why is this possible, and what is its limit? (a) \\(n=30\\): about \\(17\\) GB; \\(n=100\\): about \\(2\\times10^{31}\\) bytes, impossible. (b) The stabilizer simulator runs the \\(100\\)-qubit GHZ circuit in a fraction of a second, and every outcome is all zeros or all ones. (c) Clifford circuits keep the state in a form described by polynomially many bits (a stabilizer tableau), so they can be simulated efficiently. Adding non-Clifford gates such as \\(T\\) or arbitrary rotations removes this shortcut; a simulator’s capacity depends on the circuit’s structure, not only its width."}, {"title": "State Representations", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 State Representations A simulator can show the state hidden inside a circuit. Used carefully, this is a debugging instrument: it lets us compare the program with the predicted mathematical action before introducing sampling or hardware noise. 4.1.1 State vectors and density matrices A state vector lists complex amplitudes in a fixed basis. A density matrix also describes uncertainty and subsystems: for a pure vector ψ it is ψψ†, while a mixture averages these outer products. Statevector.from_instruction evolves an initially zero register through a suitable circuit. DensityMatrix provides the more general representation. Neither is a promise that a hardware run can reveal all those numbers. 4.1.2 Composite systems and subsystem order For a Bell pair, tracing out qubit one leaves the other in I/2. partial_trace takes the subsystem indices to discard, not the indices to keep. Its result is not the plus state even though both have fair Z measurements. The plus state has off-diagonal entries 1/2; I/2 does not. The distinction appears in an X-basis test. Problem 4.1. What does partial_trace(state,[1]) keep for two qubits? Worked solution It discards subsystem one and keeps subsystem zero. Confusing the two can pass unnoticed on symmetric states."}, {"title": "Operators and Observables", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Operators and Observables 4.2.1 Unitary operators Operator(circuit) constructs the circuit’s matrix when the circuit has a suitable unitary description. A unitary U satisfies U†U=I, and the companion checks this numerically. Matrices grow as 4^n complex entries, so this is a small-system verification technique. An exact matrix calculation is not a scalable simulation strategy for arbitrary large circuits. 4.2.2 Pauli observables and expectation values A Pauli observable has outcomes ±1. For the Bell state, XX and ZZ have expectation one, while ZI has expectation zero. SparsePauliOp stores sums of Pauli strings and coefficients without necessarily constructing a dense matrix. In a multi-qubit string, the rightmost character acts on qubit zero. The simulator computes an expectation directly; an experiment estimates it from repeated measurements. Problem 4.2. Does a zero expectation of Z imply a maximally mixed state? Worked solution No. Plus, minus, and many other states have zero Z expectation. Additional observables are needed to determine the state."}, {"title": "Simulation", "chapter": "States and Operators", "url": "04-states-and-operators.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 Simulation 4.3.1 Exact evolution and sampled results An exact expectation and a sampled mean answer different questions. The former checks ideal mathematical action; the latter models finite measurement records. A density-matrix noise simulation adds another layer. Keep all three results separately so a discrepancy can be traced to code, sampling, or the noise model rather than attributed vaguely to “quantum randomness.” 4.3.2 Simulator access versus physical measurement The companion verifies Bell correlations, the reduced state, and unitarity. Its tolerances address floating-point arithmetic. Extend it by changing H to an Ry rotation and predicting the reduced probabilities cos²(θ/2), sin²(θ/2) before running. Off-diagonal terms remain absent locally after the entangling CNOT, even though the global state is pure. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_04.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 4.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Statevector, DensityMatrix, Operator, SparsePauliOp, partial_trace\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.h(0)\nqc.cx(0, 1)\nstate = Statevector.from_instruction(qc)\nlocal = partial_trace(DensityMatrix(state), [1])\nassert np.allclose(local.data, np.eye(2) / 2)\nassert np.isclose(state.expectation_value(SparsePauliOp(\"XX\")), 1)\nassert np.isclose(state.expectation_value(SparsePauliOp(\"ZI\")), 0)\nassert np.allclose(Operator(qc).data.conj().T @ Operator(qc).data, np.eye(4))\nprint(\"Local state:\", local.data) Problem 4.3. Why avoid dense matrices in the large-instance benchmark? Worked solution Their classical construction may dominate the experiment exponentially. Use them as small-instance correctness checks, and report scalable resource counts separately. Next we give circuit fragments explicit interfaces so they can be safely reused and inverted. Sources and further study: IBM, quantum information API . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-1", "text": "The factory below appends to a module-level circuit and returns it. What does each returned object contain after two calls, and why is this a bug? Both names refer to the same circuit, which now holds two H gates, so the first result changed after it was returned. Any experiment that used it silently ran the wrong circuit. A factory should construct and return a fresh circuit on every call, as the chapter’s rotation factory does."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-2", "text": "After bound = component.assign_parameters({theta: 0.7}), which circuit still has a free parameter: component or bound? What does that imply for reusing component? component still lists theta in its parameters; bound has none. assign_parameters returns a new, bound circuit and leaves the template symbolic, so the same component can be bound to many values in a sweep without being rebuilt."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-3", "text": "Package ry(0.7) as a gate, add one control, and transpile the two-qubit circuit to the basis cx, rz, sx, x. How many CNOTs does the controlled rotation need? The transpiled circuit uses \\(2\\) CNOTs plus single-qubit gates. Adding a control turned one rotation into an entangling subcircuit; for a controlled multi-gate routine the cost grows with every gate controlled. control() is a mathematical construction with a real price, not a free flag."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-4", "text": "The chapter checks that a bound rotation composed with its inverse is the identity. (a) Show that this test also passes for a wrong rotation, ry(\\(-0.7\\)). (b) Write a test that checks the specification itself: the state prepared from \\(\\ket0\\) should be \\(\\cos(0.35)\\ket0+\\sin(0.35)\\ket1\\). (c) What does the \\(UU^\\dagger\\) test actually establish? (a) For any unitary \\(U\\), \\(UU^\\dagger=I\\), so ry(\\(-0.7\\)) passes just as ry(\\(0.7\\)) does. (b) Compare the prepared statevector with the expected amplitudes; the wrong rotation gives \\(\\cos(0.35)\\ket0-\\sin(0.35)\\ket1\\) and fails. (c) The inverse test checks only that the circuit is unitary and that inverse() is consistent with it. It says nothing about whether the circuit implements the intended operation; tests must compare against an independent specification."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-5", "text": "A predicate factory computes \\(x_0\\land x_1\\) into a scratch qubit (2) with a Toffoli, copies it into a target (3) with a CNOT, and forgets to uncompute. (a) For the inputs in uniform superposition, find the probability that the scratch qubit is left in \\(\\ket1\\). (b) Fix the factory and verify that the scratch qubit returns to \\(\\ket0\\) on this coherent input. (c) Why test with a superposition rather than with each basis input separately? (a) The scratch qubit holds \\(x_0x_1\\), so it is \\(\\ket1\\) with probability \\(\\frac14\\), and it is entangled with the inputs. (b) Applying the Toffoli again after the copy returns the scratch to \\(\\ket0\\) for every input, and its reduced state is exactly \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\). (c) Each basis input alone would show a definite scratch value and might pass a check that only looks at the target. The coherent input reveals whether the scratch factors out, which is what later interference requires."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-6", "text": "A circuit applies ry(theta) and then rz(alpha) to one qubit. A student passes the parameter values [0.0, \\(\\pi/2\\)] to an Estimator, intending theta \\(=0\\) and alpha \\(=\\pi/2\\), and measures \\(\\langle X\\rangle\\). (a) What value do they expect? (b) Qiskit orders a circuit’s parameters by name. What value is returned, and why? (c) How should the values be supplied to avoid this? (a) With theta \\(=0\\) the qubit stays \\(\\ket0\\), so \\(\\langle X\\rangle=0\\). (b) The circuit’s parameters are ordered alpha, theta, so the values assign alpha \\(=0\\) and theta \\(=\\pi/2\\): ry(\\(\\pi/2\\)) prepares \\(\\ket+\\) and the result is \\(1\\). (c) Inspect circuit.parameters before building value arrays, or bind with an explicit dictionary such as {theta: 0.0, alpha: \\(\\pi/2\\)}. Positional parameter arrays are a frequent source of silent errors."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-7", "text": "rz(\\(\\lambda\\)) and p(\\(\\lambda\\)) differ only by a global phase. Compare their controlled versions crz(0.9) and cp(0.9) with Operator equivalence. Then find the one-qubit gate on the control that makes them equal. The controlled versions are not equivalent: the phase \\(e^{-i\\lambda/2}\\) that was global for the single-qubit gate now multiplies only the control-1 branch. Adding p(\\(0.45\\)) on the control to crz(0.9) restores the factor and makes it equal to cp(0.9). A routine correct “up to global phase” must have that phase fixed before it is controlled."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student believes inverse() cannot be applied until all parameters are bound, “because the inverse angle is not known yet.” Build ry(theta).inverse() with theta unbound, bind theta afterwards, and check the result. inverse() works symbolically: it produces ry(\\(-\\)theta), whose angle is a parameter expression. Binding theta \\(=0.7\\) afterwards gives the same operator as inverting the bound circuit. Symbolic circuits can be composed, inverted, and controlled before binding, which is what lets one template serve a whole parameter sweep."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-9", "text": "Try to package a circuit that contains a measurement with to_gate(), and then with to_instruction(). Explain the difference between a gate and an instruction, and what it means for inverting or controlling the packaged object. to_gate() raises a QiskitError, because a gate represents a unitary and a measurement is not one. to_instruction() succeeds: an instruction may include classical bits and non-unitary steps. Consequently a gate can be inverted and controlled, while such an instruction generally cannot. Packaging should preserve this distinction so that later code cannot ask for an inverse that does not exist."}, {"title": "Reusable Quantum Programs — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#exercise-10", "text": "A borrowed (dirty) qubit \\(b\\) may start in any unknown state and must be returned unchanged. Show that the sequence cx(x, b), cx(b, t), cx(x, b), cx(b, t) adds \\(x\\) to the target \\(t\\) for every value of \\(b\\), and leaves \\(b\\) unchanged. Verify it on all eight basis inputs. Why is this useful? Tracking values: \\(b\\to b\\oplus x\\); \\(t\\to t\\oplus b\\oplus x\\); \\(b\\to b\\); \\(t\\to t\\oplus x\\). The unknown \\(b\\) appears twice in the target and cancels, so the sequence implements \\(t\\mathrel{\\oplus}=x\\) and restores \\(b\\), on all eight inputs and, by linearity, on superpositions. Borrowed qubits let a circuit use idle wires as temporary workspace without requiring them to be clean, saving qubits in large constructions."}, {"title": "Circuit Interfaces", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 Circuit Interfaces A useful circuit fragment should behave predictably when embedded in a larger computation. Its interface must specify wire order, parameters, and workspace, just as a classical function specifies its inputs and outputs. 5.1.1 Python circuit factories A circuit factory is a Python function returning a newly constructed circuit. The rotation factory below makes one Ry gate with a supplied angle. Fresh objects prevent one experiment’s edits from silently changing another. Returning a circuit also lets the caller decide where it is composed and when it is executed. 5.1.2 Register contracts and composition The interface of a predicate may be “input wires first, target next, zeroed scratch last; preserve inputs and clear scratch.” Document such contracts beside the factory. compose can map the component’s wires onto selected wires of a larger circuit. A valid mathematical component on the wrong wire mapping is still a program bug. Problem 5.1. Why return a fresh circuit instead of a shared global one? Worked solution Appending gates mutates circuit objects. A shared object can accumulate instructions across calls and make experiments depend on call history."}, {"title": "Reusable Operations", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Reusable Operations 5.2.1 Custom gates and controlled gates to_gate() packages a unitary circuit into a reusable gate, and control() constructs a controlled version when supported. Control is a mathematical construction whose decomposition can be costly. It is not equivalent to using a Python if statement. A global phase of the base operation becomes a relative phase when controlled and must be preserved. 5.2.2 Inverses and parameter binding Parameter(\"theta\") creates a symbolic parameter. assign_parameters binds it to a numerical value without replacing the entire circuit construction. inverse() reverses a reversible circuit; a circuit containing measurement generally has no unitary inverse. The companion composes a bound rotation with its inverse and compares the result with the identity matrix. Problem 5.2. Does U followed by U† test the intended specification? Worked solution It checks reversibility and the implementation of the inverse, but a wrong U can also pass. Compare with independently predicted outputs or matrices. A one-parameter entangled trial state: cos(θ/2)|00⟩+sin(θ/2)|11⟩. Measure ZZ directly or rotate both wires with H before measuring XX."}, {"title": "Workspace Contracts", "chapter": "Reusable Quantum Programs", "url": "05-reusable-quantum-programs.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 Workspace Contracts 5.3.1 Ancilla allocation Ancillas are extra quantum wires, not temporary Python variables. Allocate them explicitly and state their required initial state. A borrowed qubit in an unknown state is a different resource from a clean zero ancilla. Simply stopping references to a wire does not physically erase its correlation with the data. 5.3.2 Uncomputation and cleanup checks Check workspace restoration on coherent inputs, not just individual values. After a clean subroutine, the workspace must factor into its specified state, with the correct relative phases on the useful registers. A probability-zero error in scratch measurement is useful but does not alone certify the target action. The next oracle chapter gives a full coherent sign check. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_05.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 5.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nangle = Parameter(\"theta\")\ndef rotation(theta):\n    qc = QuantumCircuit(1, name=\"rotation\")\n    qc.ry(theta, 0)\n    return qc\n\ncomponent = rotation(angle)\nbound = component.assign_parameters({angle: 0.7})\nidentity = bound.compose(bound.inverse())\nassert np.allclose(Operator(identity).data, np.eye(2))\ncontrolled = bound.to_gate().control(1)\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.append(controlled, [0, 1])\nprint(qc.draw(output=\"text\")) Problem 5.3. Can a symbolic circuit be sampled before all necessary parameters are bound? Worked solution Only if parameter values are supplied through a supported execution interface. Otherwise its numerical evolution is unspecified and execution should reject it. Reusable parameterized circuits become numerical experiments through sampling and estimation primitives. Sources and further study: IBM, QuantumCircuit API ."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-1", "text": "For ry(\\(2\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\), predict \\(p(1)\\) and \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\), and check that they satisfy \\(\\langle Z\\rangle=1-2p(1)\\). \\(p(1)=\\sin^2(\\pi/3)=0.75\\) and \\(\\langle Z\\rangle=\\cos(2\\pi/3)=-0.5=1-2(0.75)\\). The Sampler would report counts near \\(1024/3072\\) for \\(4096\\) shots; the Estimator reports the mean \\(-0.5\\). They are two views of the same distribution."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-2", "text": "A sampled estimate gives \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\approx0.2\\) from \\(1000\\) shots. Find its standard error, and the shots needed to reach a standard error of \\(0.01\\). Standard error \\(\\sqrt{0.96/1000}\\approx0.031\\). Reaching \\(0.01\\) needs \\(R=0.96/10^{-4}=9600\\) shots: about ten times more for three times the precision, the familiar \\(1/\\sqrt R\\) scaling."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-3", "text": "A user passes a circuit that ends in measure_all to StatevectorEstimator together with the observable Z. What happens, and what should they pass instead? The Estimator raises a QiskitError (“Cannot apply instruction with classical bits: measure”). Expectation values are defined for the state before measurement; the Estimator handles any basis changes and sampling internally. Pass the circuit without measurements, and keep a separate measured copy for the Sampler."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-4", "text": "One Estimator PUB combines the parameterized ry circuit with observables arranged as [[Z], [X]] and parameter values [[0], [\\(\\pi/2\\)], [\\(\\pi\\)]]. (a) Predict the shape of data.evs. (b) Predict its entries. (c) Why is checking the shape part of a correct program? (a) Broadcasting the observables array of shape \\((2,1)\\) against three parameter sets gives shape \\((2,3)\\). (b) The Z row is \\(\\cos\\theta=(1,0,-1)\\) and the X row is \\(\\sin\\theta=(0,1,0)\\). (c) A different arrangement, say observables of shape \\((2,)\\), would fail to broadcast against three parameter sets, or would pair them differently. Asserting the expected shape catches workload mistakes that numerical checks on one entry would miss."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-5", "text": "A two-qubit state has qubit 0 in ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\) and qubit 1 in \\(\\ket0\\). The measurement maps qubit 0 to classical bit 1 and qubit 1 to classical bit 0. (a) Estimate \\(\\langle IZ\\rangle\\) (Z on qubit 0) from the counts, decoding the map correctly. (b) What would a student get by treating the rightmost count bit as qubit 0? (c) Does the mapping matter for \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle\\)? (a) Qubit 0’s value is in classical bit 1, the left character. Averaging \\(+1\\) for 0 and \\(-1\\) for 1 over that character gives about \\(0.5=\\cos(\\pi/3)\\). (b) The rightmost character holds qubit 1, which is always \\(0\\), so the student would report \\(1\\). (c) ZZ depends only on whether the two bits are equal, which is unchanged by swapping them, so it is immune to this mistake; single-qubit observables are not."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-6", "text": "An experiment must estimate \\(\\langle Z\\rangle+\\langle X\\rangle\\) for ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\) with \\(10\\,000\\) total shots, split between a Z-basis and an X-basis circuit. (a) Find the single-shot variances. (b) Compare the variance of the estimate for an equal split and for the allocation \\(R_j\\propto\\sigma_j\\). (c) Is the improvement worth the bookkeeping here? (a) \\(\\sigma_Z^2=1-0.25=0.75\\) and \\(\\sigma_X^2=1-0.75=0.25\\). (b) Equal split: \\(0.75/5000+0.25/5000=2.0\\times10^{-4}\\). Optimal: \\(R_Z\\approx6340\\), \\(R_X\\approx3660\\), giving \\((0.866+0.5)^2/10^4\\approx1.87\\times10^{-4}\\). (c) Only a \\(7\\%\\) reduction here. The gain becomes large when coefficients or variances differ strongly, as for Hamiltonians with many terms; the variances must themselves be estimated first."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-7", "text": "Estimate a small probability: a circuit outputs 1 with probability \\(p=0.002\\). (a) With \\(1000\\) shots, what is the expected count and the relative standard error of \\(\\hat p\\)? (b) How many shots give a \\(10\\%\\) relative error? (c) If zero ones are observed in \\(1000\\) shots, what upper bound on \\(p\\) holds at about \\(95\\%\\) confidence? (a) Expected count \\(2\\), relative error \\(\\sqrt{0.998/(0.002\\times1000)}\\approx0.71\\): the estimate is barely meaningful. (b) \\(0.1^2=0.998/(0.002R)\\) gives \\(R\\approx5\\times10^4\\). (c) About \\(3/1000=0.003\\). Small entries of a normalized count table carry large relative uncertainty, so rare outcomes need either many more shots or an estimation method designed for them."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “Doubling the shots halves the uncertainty, so going from \\(1000\\) to \\(2000\\) shots takes my standard error from \\(0.03\\) to \\(0.015\\).” Correct the claim and give the shots that actually halve it. Standard error scales as \\(1/\\sqrt R\\), so doubling the shots reduces it by \\(\\sqrt2\\), from \\(0.03\\) to about \\(0.021\\). Halving it requires four times the shots, \\(4000\\). Every extra digit of precision costs a factor of one hundred in shots, which is why measurement budgets dominate many estimation workloads."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-9", "text": "Compare two ways of running a \\(100\\)-point parameter sweep of \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) for ry(theta): one PUB with a \\((100,1)\\) array of values, and \\(100\\) separate PUBs with one value each. Show that the expectation values agree, and explain why the single-PUB form is preferable. The two runs return the same \\(100\\) values of \\(\\cos\\theta\\). A single PUB states the experiment’s structure explicitly (one circuit, one observable, a value array), lets the primitive reuse the circuit and compilation, and returns one array whose order matches the input array, reducing bookkeeping errors. Separate PUBs repeat overhead and scatter results across many objects; on remote services they may also be scheduled and billed separately."}, {"title": "Sampling and Estimation — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#exercise-10", "text": "Write a function that converts Sampler counts for two measured qubits into an estimate of any two-qubit diagonal Pauli observable (II, IZ, ZI, or ZZ), given the label and assuming the default measure_all mapping. Test it on the Bell state, where \\(\\langle ZZ\\rangle=1\\) and \\(\\langle ZI\\rangle=\\langle IZ\\rangle=0\\). Each shot contributes the product of \\(\\pm1\\) factors from the qubits where the label has Z. Since both labels and count strings put qubit 0 on the right, character \\(j\\) of the label pairs with character \\(j\\) of the bit string. On the Bell state the estimates are \\(1\\) for ZZ and close to \\(0\\) for ZI and IZ. Encoding the ordering rule once in a tested helper is safer than reimplementing it in every analysis."}, {"title": "Execution Primitives", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 Execution Primitives For Ry(θ)|0〉, the probability of one is sin²(θ/2), while the mean of Z is cos θ. These are two ways to ask about the same circuit, and they correspond to two execution interfaces. 6.1.1 V2 Sampler and Estimator A Sampler accepts measured circuits and returns shot records. An Estimator accepts circuits together with observables and returns expectation estimates. V2 interfaces group related work in primitive unified blocs, usually abbreviated PUBs. A PUB is an input grouping, not a quantum gate or a physical batch guarantee. The companion uses local Statevector implementations, so no device account is involved. 6.1.2 Local implementations and result structures The Estimator input tuple contains a circuit, an observable, and parameter values. The result is indexed by input PUB; data.evs holds the corresponding expectation array. For Sampler, data is organized by classical register name; measure_all creates meas in our examples. Explicitly named registers change the field you read. Do not paste a result-access expression without checking the circuit’s register names. Problem 6.1. At θ=π/3, predict p(1) and the Z mean. Worked solution They are 1/4 and 1/2. A result near zero would suggest a wrong angle, observable, or parameter mapping."}, {"title": "Statistical Readout", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Statistical Readout 6.2.1 Shots, precision, and uncertainty A Bernoulli frequency has variance \\(p(1-p)/R\\), while an average of ±1 values has variance \\((1-\\langle Z\\rangle^2)/R\\). The identity \\(\\langle Z\\rangle=1-2p(1)\\) connects them. Exact StatevectorEstimator at zero requested precision has no shot fluctuation for our unitary examples; it is not a model of a hardware estimator with finite shots. 6.2.2 Bit strings and classical postprocessing Decode counts using the measurement mapping before converting them into an observable. For two bits, ZZ assigns +1 to equal bits and −1 to unequal bits. Summing signed counts and dividing by total shots estimates its mean. A normalized count table is an empirical distribution; its small entries have large relative uncertainty. Problem 6.2. How does doubling shots change a standard error? Worked solution It reduces the standard error by √2, assuming independent identically distributed shots. It does not remove systematic bias."}, {"title": "Repeated Workloads", "chapter": "Sampling and Estimation", "url": "06-sampling-and-estimation.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 Repeated Workloads 6.3.1 Parameter sweeps The companion sends angles 0, π/2, and π as a three-row parameter array. Its returned Z means must be 1, 0, and −1. Parameter sweeps reuse a circuit structure while changing values. Array shapes describe the experiment and should be checked explicitly; silently broadcasting the wrong dimension can produce a different workload. 6.3.2 Batching inputs and interpreting results Batching several experiments can reduce host overhead, but each requested circuit, parameter set, observable group, and shot budget still consumes resources. Keep the parameter order and result order in the saved record. The example separates an exact three-point sweep from a sampled midpoint, so the two sources of output are unambiguous. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_06.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 6.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler, StatevectorEstimator\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import SparsePauliOp\n\ntheta = Parameter(\"theta\")\nqc = QuantumCircuit(1)\nqc.ry(theta, 0)\nvalues = [[0], [np.pi / 2], [np.pi]]\nresult = StatevectorEstimator().run([(qc, SparsePauliOp(\"Z\"), values)]).result()\nassert np.allclose(result[0].data.evs, [1, 0, -1])\nmeasured = qc.assign_parameters({theta: np.pi / 2})\nmeasured.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=9).run([measured], shots=4096).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nprint(\"Exact means:\", result[0].data.evs, \"sampled counts:\", counts) Problem 6.3. Why keep a circuit without measurements for the Estimator? Worked solution It represents the prepared state whose observable is requested. The estimation implementation chooses its measurement procedure; terminal measurements in the input can conflict with that contract. We can now run and interpret a circuit. The next step is to construct the input-dependent oracle that an algorithm assumes. Sources and further study: IBM, StatevectorEstimator ; IBM, StatevectorSampler ."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the oracle \\(f(a,b,c)=ab\\oplus c\\) (qubits 0, 1, 2 hold \\(a,b,c\\); qubit 3 is the target), which basis state does the input \\(a=b=c=1\\) with target \\(0\\) map to? Give its index. \\(f(1,1,1)=1\\oplus1=0\\), so the target stays \\(0\\) and the state is unchanged: index \\(7\\) maps to index \\(7\\). The Toffoli and the CNOT each flip the target, and the two flips cancel. Exhaustive checks over all sixteen columns catch mistakes in exactly these cancelling cases."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-2", "text": "The two-bit incrementer applies cx(0, 1) and then x(0). Tabulate its action on the four inputs \\(0,1,2,3\\). What happens to \\(3\\), and why does that keep the circuit reversible? \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to2\\), \\(2\\to3\\), and \\(3\\to0\\). The overflow wraps around: this is addition modulo \\(4\\), a permutation of the four labels, and therefore reversible. Producing \\(4\\) would need a third bit; the width is part of the function’s definition."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-3", "text": "The oracle uses one Toffoli and one CNOT. Transpile it to the basis cx, rz, sx, x with all-to-all connectivity, and then on a line of four qubits. Report the CNOT counts. With all-to-all connectivity the compiled oracle has \\(7\\) CNOTs (six for the Toffoli, one for the CNOT). On a line it needs more, about \\(10\\) in this run, because qubits 0, 1, and 3 are not all adjacent and swaps are inserted. Report both the logical count and the compiled count for a stated target."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-4", "text": "A buggy oracle appends cz(3, 0) after the correct gates. (a) Show that its output probabilities match the correct oracle on every basis input. (b) Show that the full operator comparison fails. (c) What kind of error does this represent, and which test in the chapter catches it? (a) CZ changes only signs, so every basis input produces the same output label with the same probability as the correct oracle. (b) The operators differ: the bug multiplies outputs with \\(a=1\\) and target \\(1\\) by \\(-1\\). (c) It is a phase error. When the oracle is used coherently, as in a phase-kickback algorithm, it corrupts interference. The chapter’s coherent sign test on a uniform superposition with target \\(\\ket-\\), and the full column comparison including phases, both catch it; histograms do not."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-5", "text": "Build a two-bit equality test: qubits 0, 1 hold \\(a\\), qubits 2, 3 hold \\(b\\), and qubit 4 receives \\([a=b]\\), with \\(b\\) restored. Use CNOTs to compute \\(a\\oplus b\\) into \\(b\\), X gates and a Toffoli to set the flag when both XOR bits are \\(0\\), and then uncompute. Verify all \\(16\\) inputs. Compute \\(a\\oplus b\\) into qubits 2 and 3 with two CNOTs, flip both with X, and apply a Toffoli from qubits 2, 3 to the flag, which fires exactly when both XOR bits were \\(0\\). Then undo the X gates and the CNOTs in reverse order, restoring \\(b\\). The check below confirms that every input maps to itself with the correct flag. The comparison reuses \\(b\\) as workspace instead of a separate scratch register, at the cost of a careful uncompute."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-6", "text": "For \\(f(a,b,c)=ab\\oplus c\\), the phase \\((-1)^{f}\\) factors as \\((-1)^{ab}(-1)^{c}\\). (a) Implement the phase oracle with cz(0, 1) and z(2), using no target qubit. (b) Verify that, on the uniform input with the target in \\(\\ket-\\), it produces the same state as the bit oracle. (c) What does this save? (a) cz(0, 1) multiplies by \\((-1)^{ab}\\) and z(2) by \\((-1)^c\\), together \\((-1)^{f}\\). (b) Applying them to the uniform input, with the unused target prepared in \\(\\ket-\\), gives the same state as the bit oracle with target \\(\\ket-\\). (c) It removes the Toffoli entirely (six CNOTs after compilation) and the target qubit. When a function is an XOR of simple terms, writing the phase directly is far cheaper than computing the bit and kicking it back."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-7", "text": "An oracle must apply the phase \\(e^{2\\pi i\\,0.3}\\) using a fixed-point register. (a) With \\(3\\) fractional bits, which representable value is closest to \\(0.3\\), and what is the phase error? (b) Repeat with \\(5\\) bits. (c) How many bits keep the phase error below \\(10^{-3}\\) rad? (a) \\(0.3\\times8=2.4\\), so the nearest value is \\(2/8=0.25\\), an error of \\(0.05\\) and a phase error of \\(0.31\\) rad. (b) \\(0.3\\times32=9.6\\), nearest \\(10/32=0.3125\\): error \\(0.0125\\), phase error \\(0.079\\) rad. (c) Rounding error is at most \\(2^{-m-1}\\), so the phase error is at most \\(\\pi2^{-m}\\); requiring \\(\\le10^{-3}\\) gives \\(m\\ge12\\). Each bit halves the error but adds a wire and its arithmetic."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student uses the two-bit incrementer to compute \\(3+1\\) and reports a bug when the output is \\(0\\). Is it a bug? What should the program have specified, and how would you obtain \\(4\\)? It is not a bug in the circuit: the two-bit incrementer computes \\(x+1\\bmod4\\), and \\(3+1\\equiv0\\). The bug is a mismatch between the specification and the intended use. To obtain \\(4\\), use a three-bit register (and a three-bit incrementer) or an explicit carry output; every arithmetic component must state its width and its overflow behavior."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-9", "text": "Build a three-bit incrementer using a multi-controlled X: first flip bit 2 if bits 0 and 1 are both \\(1\\), then flip bit 1 if bit 0 is \\(1\\), then flip bit 0. Verify it on all eight inputs, and explain why the order must go from the high bit down. Each bit should flip exactly when all lower bits are \\(1\\) in the original input. Processing from the high bit down ensures every controlled flip reads the lower bits before they are modified; the reverse order would read already-incremented bits. The check confirms \\(v\\to v+1\\bmod8\\) for all inputs. Larger widths need multi-controlled X gates with many controls, whose decomposition cost grows with \\(n\\)."}, {"title": "Oracles and Arithmetic — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#exercise-10", "text": "An oracle is specified only by its truth table on \\(n\\) inputs. (a) How many basis columns must an exhaustive operator check compare, including both target values? (b) For which \\(n\\) does a dense Operator check become impractical? (c) What alternative checks scale better, and what do they give up? (a) \\(2^{n+1}\\) columns (all inputs, both target values), each of length \\(2^{n+1}\\). (b) Around \\(n=12\\) to \\(14\\), the dense matrix has \\(4^{n+1}\\) entries, hundreds of millions to billions, too large for routine testing. (c) Check each basis input with a statevector or a classical reversible simulation (\\(2^{n+1}\\) runs, each cheap), plus a few coherent superposition tests for phases; or verify the construction compositionally, component by component. These scale further but no longer examine every matrix entry at once, so phase checks must be designed deliberately."}, {"title": "Reversible Predicates", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 Reversible Predicates The oracle f(a,b,c)=ab XOR c is small enough to verify exhaustively and rich enough to expose the distinction between a Boolean truth table and a coherent circuit. 7.1.1 Boolean evaluation Assign a,b,c to qubits zero, one, and two, and the answer target to qubit three. A Toffoli controlled by zero and one toggles the target by ab; CNOT from two toggles it by c. Inputs are retained and the target is XORed with f. No scratch is needed for this predicate. In a larger predicate, the compute–copy–uncompute construction supplies cleanup. 7.1.2 Answer registers and retained inputs The full contract applies for target zero and target one. Testing only a zero target can miss an implementation that initializes or overwrites the answer irreversibly. The companion checks all sixteen basis columns against the required permutation. Basis columns with their complex phases determine the whole linear operation; basis probabilities alone do not. Problem 7.1. What happens to an answer target initially one when f=1? Worked solution It becomes zero. The answer operation is XOR, not assignment."}, {"title": "Arithmetic Circuits", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 Arithmetic Circuits 7.2.1 Addition and comparison A two-bit incrementer applies CX from the low bit to the high bit, then X on the low bit, producing addition modulo four. A comparator can compute whether a−b borrows, retain the flag, and reverse the arithmetic. Explicitly choose modular or overflow-preserving behavior. The bit width is part of the function; it cannot be inferred from an unbounded mathematical integer. 7.2.2 Precision and workspace requirements Fixed-point arithmetic stores a scaled integer, so changing width changes both range and precision. Extra carries, comparison flags, and temporary products require wires and cleanup. Counting only the final phase or output gate hides most of the oracle implementation. For a finite table, state preparation or memory access also needs a specified circuit. Problem 7.2. Why test a target minus state? Worked solution It turns function values into relative phases and exposes phase or cleanup errors that may be invisible on measured basis inputs."}, {"title": "Phase Oracles", "chapter": "Oracles and Arithmetic", "url": "07-oracles-and-arithmetic.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 Phase Oracles 7.3.1 Compute, phase, and uncompute Preparing the answer target in minus converts the bit oracle to a sign oracle. Every input amplitude gains (−1)^f, while the target remains minus. The companion prepares all eight inputs uniformly and compares every final complex amplitude with this prediction. This catches unwanted phases and retained records that a histogram test could miss. 7.3.2 Superposition tests and resource counts The resource count for this implementation is one Toffoli and one CNOT, plus preparation and readout if used. A Toffoli is not native on every backend, so report both this logical count and its compiled decomposition. The circuit’s simple contract makes it usable as an independent component in search, phase encoding, or a larger arithmetic calculation. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_07.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 7.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\n# f(a,b,c) = ab XOR c; q0=a, q1=b, q2=c, q3=target.\nqc = QuantumCircuit(4)\nqc.ccx(0, 1, 3)\nqc.cx(2, 3)\nmatrix = Operator(qc).data\nfor index in range(16):\n    a, b, c = index & 1, (index >> 1) & 1, (index >> 2) & 1\n    expected = index ^ (((a & b) ^ c) << 3)\n    assert np.allclose(matrix[:, index], np.eye(16)[:, expected])\n# Target minus makes the same oracle a phase oracle.\nprep = QuantumCircuit(4)\nprep.h([0, 1, 2])\nprep.x(3)\nprep.h(3)\nstate = Statevector.from_instruction(prep.compose(qc))\nexpected = []\nfor index in range(16):\n    a, b, c, target = [(index >> k) & 1 for k in range(4)]\n    expected.append((-1)**((a & b) ^ c ^ target) / 4)\nassert np.allclose(state.data, expected)\nprint(\"All basis columns and coherent signs verified\") Problem 7.3. Is a four-bit adder an implementation of arbitrary-precision integer addition? Worked solution No. Its range and overflow behavior are fixed. A scalable implementation specifies a family parameterized by width and gives its resource growth. With verified oracles, the algorithms course can be translated into executable constructions. Sources and further study: IBM, circuit API . Phase-oracle readout. U_f is the reversible bit oracle on the input register and the minus target, not an elementary gate. The final input measurement reads the encoded pattern."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-1", "text": "Verify that the inverse of the chapter’s positive-sign QFT matches \\(e^{-2\\pi ixy/4}/2\\). Why is the sign convention worth an explicit test? qft.inverse() matches the matrix with \\(e^{-2\\pi ixy/4}/2\\). Libraries and papers differ on which sign is “the” QFT, and phase estimation needs the inverse of whichever transform produced its phase ramp. A wrong sign returns \\(-\\phi\\) (mod \\(1\\)) instead of \\(\\phi\\), an error that a test on \\(\\ket0\\) alone would miss, because both transforms send \\(\\ket0\\) to the uniform state."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Modify the chapter’s four-item search to mark \\(01\\) (qubit 0 equal to \\(1\\), qubit 1 equal to \\(0\\)) instead of \\(11\\). Which gates change, and what probability does the marked index reach after one iteration? Only the oracle changes: apply X to qubit 1, then CZ, then X to qubit 1, so the sign falls on \\(\\ket{01}\\). The diffusion step is unchanged. After one iteration, index \\(1\\) has probability \\(1\\). Separating the oracle from the diffusion makes this change local and testable."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-3", "text": "Remove the final swap from the two-qubit QFT. Show that the result equals the full QFT followed by an extra swap. Is the swap-free circuit wrong? The swap-free circuit followed by a swap equals the full QFT, so the swap-free version computes the same transform with its output qubits in reversed order. It is not wrong if the reversal is accounted for, for example by relabeling outputs or by absorbing it into later wiring; many implementations omit the swaps to save gates. It is wrong only if downstream code assumes the standard order."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-4", "text": "Implement two-bit phase estimation for \\(U=P(\\pi/2)\\), whose eigenstate \\(\\ket1\\) has phase \\(\\phi=1/4\\). Controls are qubits 0 (power \\(1\\)) and 1 (power \\(2\\)); the target is qubit 2 in \\(\\ket1\\). Apply the controlled powers as controlled phases and then the inverse of the chapter’s QFT on the controls. Predict and verify the control readout. Controlled-\\(U\\) is cp(\\(\\pi/2\\)) from qubit 0 and controlled-\\(U^2\\) is cp(\\(\\pi\\)) from qubit 1. They write \\(e^{2\\pi ik/4}\\) onto control basis state \\(\\ket k\\), and the inverse QFT returns \\(\\ket{j}\\) with \\(j=1\\). The full label is 101 (target 1, controls 01), with probability \\(1\\), so \\(\\phi=j/4=1/4\\). Testing with an exactly representable phase first isolates convention errors from rounding effects."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-5", "text": "Repeat phase estimation with three control qubits for a phase that is not exactly representable, \\(\\phi=1/3\\). (a) Which outcome is most likely, and with what probability? (b) What estimate of \\(\\phi\\) does it give? (c) How would you test this case, given that the outcome is random? (a) The control readout \\(j=3\\) has probability about \\(0.69\\), with \\(j=2\\) next at about \\(0.17\\). (b) \\(j=3\\) gives \\(\\phi\\approx3/8=0.375\\), the nearest grid value to \\(1/3\\). (c) Compare the exact output distribution from Statevector with the analytic formula for every \\(j\\), rather than asserting a single outcome; in sampled runs, test that the frequency of \\(j=3\\) lies within a tolerance of \\(0.69\\)."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-6", "text": "Implement a coined walk on a four-cycle: qubit 0 is the coin, qubits 1 and 2 hold the position (low bit on qubit 1). Each step applies H to the coin, increments the position if the coin is \\(1\\), and decrements it if the coin is \\(0\\). Starting from coin \\(0\\) at position \\(0\\), predict the position distribution after three steps and verify it. After three steps the walker is at position \\(3\\) with certainty: interference cancels every route to position \\(1\\). The controlled increment is ccx(0, 1, 2), cx(0, 1); the decrement controlled on coin \\(0\\) is X on the coin, then cx(0, 1), ccx(0, 1, 2), then X on the coin. A classical random walk would be at position \\(1\\) or \\(3\\) with probability \\(\\frac12\\) each; the coin register must be kept, not measured, for the interference to occur."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-7", "text": "Implement Grover search for one marked item, \\(111\\), among eight. Use H, Toffoli, H on qubit 2 as the controlled-controlled-Z oracle, and the standard diffusion. Compute the success probability after \\(0,1,2,3\\) iterations and compare with \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\), \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt8\\). The success probabilities are \\(0.125\\), \\(0.781\\), \\(0.945\\), and \\(0.330\\) for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\), exactly \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\). The third iteration overshoots. The test compares each value with the formula, which checks both the oracle and the diffusion, and documents why the iteration count is a parameter derived from \\(N\\), not a loop that runs until some budget is exhausted."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student tests a QFT implementation only on the input \\(\\ket{00}\\), checks that the output is the uniform superposition, and declares it correct. Construct a wrong circuit that passes this test, and state a test that would catch it. \\(H\\otimes H\\) alone sends \\(\\ket{00}\\) to the uniform superposition, exactly like the QFT, and so does the inverse QFT. All three differ on other inputs; for example the QFT and \\(H\\otimes H\\) give different phases on \\(\\ket{01}\\). A correct test compares the full operator with the defining matrix (as the chapter does), or checks every basis input including relative phases. A single input exercises only one column of the matrix."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-9", "text": "Build a four-qubit positive-sign QFT and an approximate version that omits the smallest controlled phase, cp(\\(\\pi/8\\)). (a) Compute the operator-norm distance between them. (b) Compare it with the bound \\(|e^{i\\pi/8}-1|\\). (c) How does this scale to large registers? (a) The distance is about \\(0.390\\). (b) That equals \\(|e^{i\\pi/8}-1|\\approx0.390\\): removing one controlled phase changes the circuit by exactly that gate’s deviation from the identity, because unitaries before and after it preserve norms. (c) Dropping every rotation smaller than \\(2\\pi/2^b\\) gives total error below about \\(2\\pi n/2^b\\) with only \\(O(n\\log n)\\) gates, the approximate QFT of Quantum Algorithms; omitting larger angles, as here, gives a large error."}, {"title": "Algorithm Implementation — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#exercise-10", "text": "In phase estimation with \\(m\\) control qubits, controlled-\\(U^{2^j}\\) built by repetition needs \\(2^j\\) uses of controlled-\\(U\\). (a) Count the total uses for \\(m=3\\) and \\(m=10\\). (b) For \\(U=P(\\lambda)\\), what shortcut implements \\(U^{2^j}\\) at the cost of one gate? (c) Why does a benchmark of phase estimation need to state which of these was used? (a) \\(m=3\\): \\(7\\) uses; \\(m=10\\): \\(1023\\). (b) \\(P(\\lambda)^{2^j}=P(2^j\\lambda)\\), a single controlled phase, which is why the worked problems used cp(\\(2^j\\lambda\\)) directly. (c) With repetition, the circuit grows exponentially in the number of bits of precision; with a shortcut, linearly. Modular multiplication in Shor’s algorithm has such a shortcut, while general Hamiltonian evolution does not. A runtime or gate count is meaningless without saying which construction was used."}, {"title": "Fourier Methods", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 Fourier Methods Implement two familiar transformations and compare their matrices or output probabilities with independent formulas. Small examples are useful for debugging, but the construction must keep its conventions explicit. 8.1.1 Constructing the Fourier circuit Our two-qubit Fourier transform uses the positive exponential exp(2πixy/4)/2. The code applies H to qubit one, a controlled π/2 phase, H to qubit zero, and a swap. The matrix assertion compares every entry with the mathematical transform. Omitting the swap changes output bit order; taking the inverse changes the exponent sign. 8.1.2 Controlled powers and phase estimation Phase estimation prepares a control register, applies controlled powers of a chosen unitary, then inverse QFT. A power implemented by repeating U costs that many U uses. When testing, choose an eigenstate with an exactly representable phase before testing approximate phases or superpositions. Decode the control bits using the register mapping, not the visual wire order alone. Problem 8.1. Why compare QFT matrices instead of only transforming zero? Worked solution Both forward and inverse Fourier transforms map zero to the same uniform state. A zero-input test misses the sign convention. Positive-sign two-qubit Fourier circuit. The final SWAP exchanges the two wires; its connected endpoints are a two-wire operation. The controlled phase applies only when both bits are one."}, {"title": "Amplitude Manipulation", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 Amplitude Manipulation 8.2.1 Preparation and reflections For four items with only 11 marked, begin uniformly and apply CZ to negate the marked amplitude. A prepared-state reflection follows. The reflection sequence used in the companion differs by a global sign from one common convention; the success probability is unaffected. This phase convention would matter if the whole iteration were later coherently controlled. 8.2.2 Search and amplification One Grover iteration gives unit success for one marked item out of four. The companion checks probability at index three. For larger instances, choose iterations from the initial success probability or use a justified unknown-success schedule. More iterations can reduce success, so a loop that runs “until enough gates” is not an algorithm. Problem 8.2. What happens after a second Grover iteration in the four-item example? Worked solution The success returns to one quarter. The rotation has passed the good direction."}, {"title": "Quantum Walks", "chapter": "Algorithm Implementation", "url": "08-algorithm-implementation.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 Quantum Walks 8.3.1 Position and coin registers A coined walk uses a coin register and a position register. Implement a coin rotation followed by reversible conditional increment and decrement modulo the graph size. Keep the coin: discarding its direction information after each step changes the evolution. A non-power-of-two position space also needs a policy for unused basis states so the full circuit remains unitary. 8.3.2 Implementation and classical baselines A meaningful benchmark compares the same access and output tasks. Verify small matrices, then measure compiled gates, depth, qubits, oracle calls, and readout costs as size grows. A classical simulator runtime is a property of the simulation method, not evidence that the quantum algorithm is slow or fast on quantum hardware. The two examples below establish correctness checks, not a performance advantage. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_08.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 8.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\n# Positive-sign QFT on integer labels |q1 q0>.\nqft = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqft.h(1)\nqft.cp(np.pi / 2, 0, 1)\nqft.h(0)\nqft.swap(0, 1)\nexpected = np.array([[np.exp(2j*np.pi*x*y/4)/2 for x in range(4)] for y in range(4)])\nassert np.allclose(Operator(qft).data, expected)\n# One Grover step for marked state |11> in four items.\nsearch = QuantumCircuit(2)\nsearch.h([0, 1])\nsearch.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.h([0, 1]); search.x([0, 1]); search.cz(0, 1)\nsearch.x([0, 1]); search.h([0, 1])\nassert np.isclose(Statevector.from_instruction(search).probabilities()[3], 1)\nprint(qft.draw(output=\"text\")) Problem 8.3. Can the position register alone generally implement a reversible coined shift? Worked solution The direction label is needed to distinguish the routes. Removing it without another reversible encoding can merge states and invalidate the proposed unitary. The examples suggest a testing discipline. The next chapter makes that discipline systematic. Sources and further study: IBM, quantum information operators ."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-1", "text": "A circuit should do nothing to one qubit, but a bug inserts an S gate. Which of these tests detects the bug: (a) basis-state output probabilities for \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\); (b) statevector equivalence on \\(\\ket+\\); (c) Operator equivalence? (a) Misses it: S leaves the basis probabilities unchanged. (b) Catches it: \\(S\\ket+=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) is not equivalent to \\(\\ket+\\). (c) Catches it: diag\\((1,i)\\) is not the identity up to a global phase. Tests of coherent components must include superposition inputs or full operators."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-2", "text": "Why does np.isclose(1e-17, 0, rtol=1e-9, atol=0) return False, while np.isclose(1e-17, 0, rtol=0, atol=1e-12) returns True? Which tolerance should an amplitude test use? With atol=0, the allowed difference is rtol times the magnitude of the reference value, which is \\(0\\), so even \\(10^{-17}\\) fails. An absolute tolerance of \\(10^{-12}\\) accepts floating-point noise. Amplitude tests should include an absolute tolerance, because many correct amplitudes are exactly zero and their computed values are tiny nonzero numbers (such as the \\(10^{-33}\\) entries seen in earlier chapters)."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-3", "text": "rz(0.4) and p(0.4) differ by a global phase. Does np.allclose on their Operator matrices succeed? Does Operator.equiv? Which comparison answers “are these the same standalone gate?” np.allclose fails, because every entry differs by the factor \\(e^{-0.2i}\\). Operator.equiv succeeds, because it allows a global phase. equiv answers the standalone question; allclose answers the stricter question that matters when the gate will be controlled, where the two are genuinely different. A test must use the notion of equality that matches the gate’s intended use."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-4", "text": "Write a test that compares an implementation with its specification on a few random input states, using random_statevector with fixed seeds. Apply it to the S-gate bug of the quick check. Why are random states useful, and what do they not replace? For each seeded random state, evolve it through both circuits and require equivalence; the S bug fails on every generic input, since a random state has both basis components. Random inputs exercise all relative phases at once and catch errors that special inputs such as \\(\\ket0\\) hide. They do not replace an exhaustive operator check for small components, and they are not a proof: a bug confined to a tiny part of a large space may need many samples or a structured test."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-5", "text": "A test suite runs \\(20\\) independent sampling tests, each with a two-sided tolerance of \\(z\\) standard deviations. (a) For \\(z=3\\), each correct test fails with probability about \\(0.0027\\). What is the chance that a run of the whole suite has at least one false failure? (b) Repeat for \\(z=4\\) (each \\(6.3\\times10^{-5}\\)). (c) What else should be done instead of simply raising \\(z\\)? (a) \\(1-0.9973^{20}\\approx5.3\\%\\): one in twenty runs of a correct suite fails. (b) \\(1-(1-6.3\\times10^{-5})^{20}\\approx0.13\\%\\). (c) Larger tolerances reduce false alarms but also hide small bugs. Better: test deterministic properties exactly with Statevector or Operator, use fixed seeds for regression tests of sampling code, and size the shot counts so that the bugs that matter remain detectable at the chosen \\(z\\)."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-6", "text": "A product-formula simulation of \\(e^{-i(X+Z)t}\\) for \\(t=1\\) with \\(r=10\\) first-order steps has the error bound \\(t^2\\|[X,Z]\\|/(2r)=0.1\\). Write a test that compares the circuit’s operator with the exact exponential and uses this analytic bound as the tolerance. Check the actual error. The circuit repeats ten steps of \\(e^{-iX\\delta}e^{-iZ\\delta}\\), implemented as rz(\\(2\\delta\\)) then rx(\\(2\\delta\\)), since \\(R_x(\\theta)=e^{-i\\theta X/2}\\). The exact unitary comes from diagonalizing \\(X+Z\\). The operator-norm error is about \\(0.07\\), within the bound \\(0.1\\), so the test passes. A tight tolerance such as \\(10^{-12}\\) would wrongly fail this correct approximate circuit; the tolerance must come from the approximation’s error budget."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-7", "text": "A biased-coin bug shifts \\(P(1)\\) from \\(0.50\\) to \\(0.48\\). How many shots are needed for a test with a three-standard-deviation false-alarm threshold to detect this bug with \\(90\\%\\) probability? Use \\(N\\approx\\big((z_\\alpha+z_\\beta)\\sigma/\\delta\\big)^2\\) with \\(z_\\alpha=3\\), \\(z_\\beta=1.28\\), \\(\\sigma=0.5\\), \\(\\delta=0.02\\). \\(N\\approx107^2\\approx1.1\\times10^4\\) shots. With the \\(4096\\) shots of earlier examples, the test would usually miss a \\(2\\%\\) bias. Detection power must be planned: decide the smallest error that matters, then choose shots. When the property is deterministic, an exact Statevector check detects the bias without any shots."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “If a test passes with a large tolerance, the circuit is correct; larger tolerances are just safer.” Using the S-gate bug, show a tolerance at which a statevector comparison on \\(\\ket+\\) would pass, and explain why “safer” is the wrong word. The outputs \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(S\\ket+\\) have fidelity \\(\\frac12\\); a test that accepts any fidelity above \\(0.4\\), or any amplitude difference below \\(1\\), would pass the bug. A tolerance is not a safety margin: it defines which differences count as errors. It must be larger than numerical noise or approximation error, and much smaller than the smallest bug the test exists to catch. Too large is as wrong as too small."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-9", "text": "A subroutine is supposed to act only on qubits 0 and 1 of a three-qubit register. Write a test that verifies its Operator equals \\(I\\otimes U_{\\rm sub}\\), so that qubit 2 is untouched. What kinds of bugs does this catch that tests of the two-qubit block alone would not? Comparing Operator(big) with Operator(I).tensor(Operator(sub)) checks that the embedded circuit acts as the subroutine on qubits 0, 1 and as the identity on qubit 2. It catches wrong wire mappings, gates accidentally applied to spectator qubits, and entanglement with the spectator (for example a stray CNOT onto qubit 2), none of which a test of the subroutine in isolation would see. The tensor order follows Qiskit’s convention that the left factor acts on the higher index."}, {"title": "Testing and Verification — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#exercise-10", "text": "Mutation testing measures a test suite by injecting small bugs. For the Bell circuit, consider four mutants: swap control and target; append Z on qubit 0; replace H by SX; drop the CNOT. Compare two tests: (a) “the counts contain only 00 and 11”; (b) statevector equivalence with \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). How many mutants does each detect? Swapping control and target, or dropping the CNOT, produces outcomes 00 and 01, so test (a) catches both. Appending Z gives \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), and SX gives \\((\\ket{00}-i\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\): both still give only 00 and 11, so test (a) misses them. Test (b) catches all four. Mutation testing turns “my tests pass” into a measurable statement about which bugs they would catch, and shows that phase errors need phase-sensitive tests."}, {"title": "Functional Tests", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 Functional Tests A wrong circuit can pass every computational-basis probability test. Identity and Z are the simplest example: they preserve basis labels but disagree on plus. Verification must test the kind of behavior the component promises. 9.1.1 Basis inputs and superpositions A basis test should compare complex output amplitudes when checking a coherent contract. If it compares only measured labels, it may miss input-dependent phases. Superpositions make those phases visible. The companion first confirms that I and Z have identical basis output probabilities, then shows that their actions on plus are inequivalent. 9.1.2 Equivalence and global phase A whole circuit can differ by one global phase without changing its standalone output statistics. Operator.equiv recognizes that freedom. An independent phase on each input column is not a global phase. Moreover, controlling a unitary turns its global phase into a relative control phase. The companion checks both cases, so a tolerance or equivalence shortcut does not erase meaningful distinctions. Problem 9.1. Why does testing Z on zero miss the bug? Worked solution Zero is an eigenstate of Z with eigenvalue one. Test plus to expose the relative sign between zero and one."}, {"title": "State and Workspace Checks", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 State and Workspace Checks 9.2.1 Register ordering and ancilla cleanup Use asymmetric inputs to test ordering. Check the probability of nonzero scratch outcomes, the reduced target state, and the final joint state when small enough. A clean workspace should factor from the useful registers. Basis tests for a fixed initialized workspace certify only that subspace, which is appropriate if the interface explicitly requires that initialization. 9.2.2 Inverse and composition tests Test inverse and composition identities, but pair them with a specification test. U followed by its computed inverse is identity even if U implements the wrong function. Composition can also reveal incompatible wire contracts or omitted phase conventions. Random complex inputs are useful additional checks, not a substitute for a proof or a complete small matrix test. Problem 9.2. Can two individually global-equivalent gates always be substituted under control? Worked solution No. Their differing phase acts only in the control-one branch and becomes observable relative to the control-zero branch."}, {"title": "Statistical Tests", "chapter": "Testing and Verification", "url": "09-testing-and-verification.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 Statistical Tests 9.3.1 Numerical tolerances Numerical tolerances should reflect floating-point accumulation and approximation error. Exact small gates can use tight absolute tolerances such as 10^-12; an intentionally approximate product formula needs its analytic error budget. Relative tolerance alone is unsuitable near a zero amplitude. Report which equivalence notion and norm the test uses. 9.3.2 Sampling uncertainty and regression tests Sampling tests need statistical tolerances and can occasionally fail even for correct code. Fix seeds for regression reproducibility, but also check deterministic state or operator predictions. Avoid demanding exact counts from an ideal probabilistic experiment. The companion intentionally includes a wrong circuit and verifies that the meaningful coherent test rejects it. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_09.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 9.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator, Statevector\n\nidentity = QuantumCircuit(1)\nwrong = QuantumCircuit(1)\nwrong.z(0)\n# Basis output probabilities match, but relative phase differs.\nfor label in (\"0\", \"1\"):\n    state = Statevector.from_label(label)\n    assert np.allclose(state.evolve(identity).probabilities(), state.evolve(wrong).probabilities())\nassert not Statevector.from_label(\"+\").evolve(identity).equiv(Statevector.from_label(\"+\").evolve(wrong))\nassert not Operator(identity).equiv(Operator(wrong))\nphase = QuantumCircuit(1)\nphase.global_phase = 0.3\nassert Operator(identity).equiv(Operator(phase))\nassert not Operator(identity.to_gate().control()).equiv(Operator(phase.to_gate().control()))\nprint(\"Relative-phase bug detected; controlled global phase also checked\") Problem 9.3. Is a larger tolerance always safer? Worked solution It reduces false failures but can accept real bugs. Choose it from the numerical and approximation budgets, and include known incorrect examples that should fail. The next transformation is performed by a compiler. Its output needs the same semantic checks, with layout taken into account."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-1", "text": "The companion compiles H on qubit 0 and CX from 0 to 2 for a line of three qubits. How many CNOTs does the compiled circuit contain, and where do the extra ones come from? The compiled circuit has \\(4\\) CNOTs: three implement a SWAP that brings the logical qubits together, and one is the requested CNOT. The logical circuit had one. Connectivity, not the algorithm, accounts for three quarters of the entangling gates here."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-2", "text": "A circuit applies H twice to qubit 0 and then CX from 0 to 1. Compare the compiled gate counts at optimization levels 0 and 1. What does the comparison show, and what does an optimization level not guarantee? Level 0 keeps both Hadamards (decomposed into several rz and sx gates) plus the CNOT; level 1 cancels them and leaves just the CNOT. Higher levels apply more passes, which usually reduces counts, but no level guarantees the smallest possible circuit, or even a smaller one than a lower level, for every input. Compare results and report the level used."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-3", "text": "A device supports CNOT only from qubit 0 to qubit 1. Show that Hadamards on both qubits before and after a CX(0, 1) implement CX(1, 0). What does a wrong-direction CNOT cost? \\((H\\otimes H)\\,\\mathrm{CX}_{0\\to1}\\,(H\\otimes H)=\\mathrm{CX}_{1\\to0}\\), which Operator equivalence confirms. A reversed CNOT therefore costs four extra Hadamards (or their native equivalents). Direction constraints, like connectivity, are part of the target and appear in compiled counts and depth."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-4", "text": "For the companion’s compiled circuit, (a) compare Operator(compiled) directly with Operator(circuit); (b) compare Operator.from_circuit(compiled) with Operator(circuit); (c) inspect the final layout. Explain the results. (a) The direct comparison fails. (b) The layout-aware comparison succeeds. (c) The final index layout is \\([0,2,1]\\): after routing, logical qubits 1 and 2 have exchanged physical positions, because the inserted SWAP was not undone. A matrix built on physical indices therefore differs from the logical operator by that permutation. Equivalence checks of compiled circuits must account for layout, or they report false errors (or, worse, hide real ones)."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-5", "text": "Recompile the companion with initial_layout=[0, 2, 1], placing logical qubit 2 on physical qubit 1. Predict the CNOT count, and explain why layout is part of a resource estimate. With logical qubits 0 and 2 placed on adjacent physical qubits 0 and 1, the CNOT is native and no routing is needed: \\(1\\) CNOT instead of \\(4\\). The same logical circuit can cost four times more depending on placement, so a reported count must state the target, the layout (or the layout method), and the seed."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compile an arbitrary single-qubit unitary (a seeded random_unitary wrapped in UnitaryGate) to the basis rz, sx, x. How many gates result? Why can every single-qubit unitary be written with at most two SX gates and three RZ gates? The compiled circuit has \\(3\\) RZ and \\(2\\) SX gates. Every single-qubit unitary has an Euler decomposition \\(R_z(\\alpha)R_y(\\beta)R_z(\\gamma)\\) up to global phase, and a Y rotation can be written as RZ rotations sandwiching SX gates, giving the pattern RZ, SX, RZ, SX, RZ. Arbitrary single-qubit gates are cheap on this basis; two-qubit gates and routing dominate compiled costs."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-7", "text": "A four-qubit circuit applies a CNOT between every pair of qubits (six CNOTs). Compile it for all-to-all connectivity and for a line. Compare the CNOT counts, and estimate the failure contribution of the extra gates if each CNOT fails with probability \\(5\\times10^{-3}\\). All-to-all: \\(6\\) CNOTs. Line: about \\(11\\) in this run, nearly double, because non-neighbouring pairs need routing. The extra five CNOTs add roughly \\(5\\times5\\times10^{-3}=2.5\\%\\) failure probability. Interaction patterns that are dense in qubit pairs pay heavily on sparse hardware unless the algorithm is restructured, for example with the swap networks of Quantum Hardware Chapter 17."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student compiles a circuit for one device and then runs the compiled circuit on a different device “because it is already in native gates.” Name three reasons the compiled circuit may be invalid or poor on the second device. The second device may support a different native gate set (for example a different entangling gate), have different connectivity or allowed CNOT directions, so the routed circuit uses pairs it cannot couple, and have different error rates, so a layout chosen to avoid bad qubits on the first device may land on bad qubits on the second. A compiled circuit is specific to its target; keep the logical circuit and recompile for each backend."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-9", "text": "Full Operator equivalence checks are impossible for large compiled circuits. Propose a scalable check and implement a small version: compare the compiled and logical circuits on several random product input states, accounting for the layout. What can such a check miss? For each trial, prepare a random product state on the logical qubits, run it through the logical circuit, and through the compiled circuit with inputs and outputs permuted by the layout; then compare. Statevector simulation scales further than dense matrices, and product inputs are easy to prepare. The small implementation below checks the companion’s circuit this way. Random tests can miss errors confined to a small subspace, so for critical components they should complement structured proofs rather than replace them."}, {"title": "Compilation and Transpilation — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#exercise-10", "text": "Explain what seed_transpiler controls, and design a fair comparison between two compilation strategies that both use randomized passes. What should be reported alongside the best circuit found? seed_transpiler fixes the pseudorandom choices inside randomized passes, such as trial layouts and routing decisions, making a compilation reproducible with the same software version. A fair comparison runs both strategies over the same set of seeds (or the same number of trials) on the same target, and reports the distribution of results (mean, spread, best), the number of seeds tried, the compilation time, and the software versions. Reporting only the best of many seeds for one strategy and a single seed for the other overstates the difference."}, {"title": "Backend Targets", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 Backend Targets A circuit requests CNOT between qubits zero and two, but the device has only a line of neighboring qubits. Compilation must change the implementation while preserving the logical computation. 10.1.1 Native operations A backend target describes supported operations and the qubits on which they can act, often with timing and calibration information. A list of native gate names is not enough to describe all device constraints. The example uses a simple line coupling graph and an explicit gate basis so the routing problem is visible without contacting hardware. 10.1.2 Connectivity and device constraints Connectivity can force swaps or equivalent routing operations. Native entangling directions may also require extra gates. These costs depend on both the circuit and the chosen placement. Logical qubits are variables in the algorithm; physical qubits are execution locations. Their correspondence can change during compilation. Problem 10.1. Why may a native gate count exceed a logical count? Worked solution A logical gate may require decomposition or routing. The two counts use different operation vocabularies."}, {"title": "Transpilation", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Transpilation 10.2.1 Decomposition and synthesis Decomposition breaks a composite operation into smaller pieces. Synthesis constructs a sequence approximating a specified transformation within an error target. Transpilation can combine these with layout, routing, and simplification. Optimization levels select strategies; a larger level is not a proof of a smaller or better circuit for every instance. 10.2.2 Layout, routing, and optimization An initial layout assigns logical wires to physical positions. Routing may leave a final permutation. Comparing compiled matrices without accounting for that mapping can report a false error. The companion uses Operator.from_circuit so the circuit’s layout is interpreted, then checks equivalence with the original logical operator. Problem 10.2. What does an optimization level guarantee? Worked solution It selects a compilation strategy; it does not certify a globally minimal circuit or better physical performance on every backend."}, {"title": "Compiler Inspection", "chapter": "Compilation and Transpilation", "url": "10-compilation-and-transpilation.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 Compiler Inspection 10.3.1 Pass managers A pass manager organizes analysis and transformation passes. Fixing seed_transpiler makes randomized choices reproducible within the tested implementation. Inspect the resulting layout, operation counts, and depth rather than treating compilation as a black box. Run several seeds only if that search effort is included in the reported classical cost. 10.3.2 Logical equivalence and physical resource counts The example compiles a three-qubit circuit to rz, sx, x, and cx on a line. It prints logical and compiled counts and checks semantic equivalence. For larger circuits, full matrices may be impossible; use component proofs, structured tests, and observable checks with stated limits. Resource estimates should include added ancillas, routing, synthesis precision, and the target model. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_10.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 10.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\nfrom qiskit.transpiler import CouplingMap\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3)\nqc.h(0)\nqc.cx(0, 2)\ncompiled = transpile(qc, basis_gates=[\"rz\", \"sx\", \"x\", \"cx\"],\n    coupling_map=CouplingMap.from_line(3), initial_layout=[0, 1, 2],\n    optimization_level=1, seed_transpiler=19)\nlogical = Operator.from_circuit(compiled)\nassert logical.equiv(Operator(qc))\nprint(\"Logical\", qc.count_ops(), \"compiled\", compiled.count_ops())\nprint(\"Layout:\", compiled.layout) Problem 10.3. Why record the layout with an experiment? Worked solution It tells which physical wires realize the logical ones and is needed to interpret observables and sometimes output mappings correctly. Compilation prepares a device-compatible workload. Hardware execution adds account access, budgets, and provenance. Sources and further study: IBM, transpiler API . Bell-pair preparation. A Hadamard followed by CNOT makes (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. The first wire is the control."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-1", "text": "A three-qubit GHZ circuit is compiled for a two-qubit synthetic backend, GenericBackendV2(2). What happens, and what should the program check before compiling for a real backend? The pass manager raises a TranspilerError (“Number of qubits greater than device”). Before compiling, a program should check width, supported instructions (including any dynamic-circuit features), and connectivity, and fail with a clear message rather than attempt a submission that cannot run."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-2", "text": "List the information that should be saved with every hardware job so that its results can be interpreted and compared later. Save the job identifier immediately; the backend name; submission and execution times; package versions; the compiled (ISA) circuit and its layout; shots and primitive options; any mitigation settings; the returned metadata; and the raw results. Device calibrations drift, so without this record a later run, or the same circuit on another device, cannot be compared meaningfully."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-3", "text": "A study plans \\(50\\) circuits with \\(4096\\) shots each, and each shot takes about \\(200\\) μs on the device, including reset. Estimate the device time. Why is this only a lower bound on the cost? About \\(50\\times4096\\times2\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm s\\approx41\\) s of shot time. Actual usage also includes per-job and per-circuit overhead, classical processing, possible repetitions, and calibration circuits for mitigation; queue time adds wall-clock delay. Budgets should be estimated before submission rather than discovered afterwards."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-4", "text": "A circuit flips logical qubit 0 and is compiled with initial_layout [2, 0, 1], so logical qubit 0 sits on physical qubit 2. (a) Evaluate the logical observable IIZ (Z on logical qubit 0) on the compiled circuit without remapping. (b) Remap it with apply_layout and evaluate again. (c) Which value is correct? (a) Unmapped, IIZ measures physical qubit 0, which holds logical qubit 1 in \\(\\ket0\\): the result is \\(+1\\). (b) apply_layout turns it into ZII, Z on physical qubit 2, and the result is \\(-1\\). (c) \\(-1\\) is correct: logical qubit 0 was flipped. An Estimator workload on a compiled circuit must use observables mapped through the layout, or it silently measures the wrong qubits."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-5", "text": "Save a compiled circuit for provenance using Qiskit’s QPY format, reload it, and check that the reloaded circuit equals the original. Why store the compiled circuit rather than only the source code that produced it? QPY serialization round-trips the compiled circuit exactly, including parameters and layout information, and the reloaded circuit compares equal. Storing the compiled circuit records what actually ran: recompiling the same source later can give a different layout or gate sequence, because of different software versions, different backend calibrations, or seeds. Results are about executed circuits, not intended ones."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-6", "text": "Compile the Bell circuit for GenericBackendV2(2, seed=3) and inspect the operation counts. Which native gates appear, and how many two-qubit gates does the ISA circuit contain? The backend’s operations include cx, rz, sx, x, reset, delay, and measure. The compiled Bell circuit contains one CNOT, two RZ, one SX, a barrier, and two measurements: the Hadamard was rewritten in RZ and SX. Inspecting the ISA circuit before submission confirms that only supported instructions remain and gives the counts that a resource estimate needs."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-7", "text": "Simulate the Bell circuit on a noisy model of the synthetic backend (AerSimulator.from_backend). Estimate the fraction of shots giving 00 or 11 with its standard error. What does this number describe, and what does it not? With \\(4000\\) shots the success fraction is about \\(0.99\\) with a standard error near \\(0.0015\\). It describes this synthetic noise model under these settings: the error rates of GenericBackendV2 are generated, not measured on any device. It does not predict a real backend’s performance, and population success alone would not distinguish a Bell state from a classical mixture; that needs a second measurement basis."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student reports that “the device gave \\(99\\%\\) Bell fidelity,” based on a run with GenericBackendV2. What is wrong with the claim, and what would a hardware claim need? GenericBackendV2 is a synthetic backend with generated properties; no device was involved, so nothing was measured about real hardware. The quantity measured, the fraction of 00 and 11, is a population success rate, not a fidelity. A hardware claim needs the device name and date, the compiled circuit and layout, shots and uncertainty, a fidelity estimate that includes coherence (for example from \\(XX\\) and \\(YY\\) measurements as well as \\(ZZ\\)), and a statement of any mitigation applied."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-9", "text": "Teaching code must never launch hardware work by accident. Write a small guard that raises an error unless submission is explicitly enabled and an exact backend name is given, and test both branches without any account access. The guard returns “local” when submission is not requested, raises a ValueError when submission is requested without a backend name, and returns the backend name otherwise; only then would the caller construct a service and submit. Testing the guard needs no credentials. Keeping the decision in one tested function makes the submission boundary explicit, as the chapter’s companion does with --submit and --backend."}, {"title": "Quantum Hardware Execution — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#exercise-10", "text": "A parameter sweep of \\(100\\) values is to be run on hardware. Compare (a) \\(100\\) separate jobs, each with one bound circuit; (b) one job with a single PUB containing the parameterized circuit and a \\(100\\)-row value array. Which costs are reduced by (b), and which are not? What must still be recorded per parameter value? Option (b) compiles once, submits once, and incurs per-job overhead and queue time once, and its results arrive as one array in parameter order. It does not reduce the shots per value or the device time spent on them. Each value’s result must still be linked to its parameter and to the job’s metadata, and if calibration drifts during a long job, the timing within the job matters too."}, {"title": "Execution Setup", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-1", "text": "11.1 Execution Setup The same Bell circuit can be tested locally and submitted remotely. The two executions need different claims: a local simulation predicts an ideal model, while a hardware result reports a particular device at a particular time. 11.1.1 Backend selection and credentials Choose an accessible backend that supports the circuit’s width, instructions, and control requirements. Account setup and credentials belong outside source files. The companion uses a synthetic local backend by default. It does not access an account or submit a job unless the user explicitly supplies --submit and an exact --backend name. 11.1.2 Executable circuits and mapped observables Compile against the selected backend’s target before submission. For an Estimator workload, map each logical observable through the compiled circuit’s layout, for example with observable.apply_layout(compiled.layout). Otherwise the observable may refer to the wrong physical wires. The example uses a Sampler, whose measurement mapping is carried by the compiled circuit. Problem 11.1. Does importing the Runtime package submit a circuit? Worked solution No. A run call on a remote execution object is the submission action. Keep that action separated from module import and local testing."}, {"title": "Runtime Workloads", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-2", "text": "11.2 Runtime Workloads 11.2.1 Submitting and retrieving jobs A Runtime Sampler is constructed with the selected backend as its mode. Calling run submits the workload and returns a job handle. Save the job identifier immediately so a later process can recover the record. The submit branch below uses configured account access; it has not been run on hardware for this course edition. 11.2.2 Execution modes and budgets Job, session, and batch modes organize execution differently. Their availability and accounting depend on the service and account. Estimate circuits, shots, and expected device usage before submission; do not infer a fixed free quota. A short Python statement can launch substantial work. Keep the explicit submission boundary visible in reusable teaching code. Problem 11.2. Why map an Estimator observable after layout? Worked solution The logical qubits may have moved. Measuring Z on a different physical qubit can answer a different question even when the compiled state is correct."}, {"title": "Result Analysis", "chapter": "Quantum Hardware Execution", "url": "11-quantum-hardware-execution.html#section-11-3", "text": "11.3 Result Analysis 11.3.1 Metadata and provenance Save the backend name, execution time, package versions, compiled circuit, layout, shots, options, and returned metadata. A count table without those facts cannot be reliably compared with another run. Device calibration drifts, so a later result may differ even with the same source circuit. Report what was actually executed rather than an intended logical circuit alone. 11.3.2 Simulation, hardware, and uncertainty The local path compiles against GenericBackendV2 and prints its resource counts. The remote path constructs an ISA circuit, submits 1024 shots, prints the job ID, and retrieves counts. Only the local path is included in automated checks. No hardware outcomes are invented; the example’s Bell-state ideal prediction remains 00 and 11 with equal probability, while a device may also produce errors. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_11.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 11.\"\"\"\nimport argparse\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.transpiler import generate_preset_pass_manager\nfrom qiskit.providers.fake_provider import GenericBackendV2\nfrom qiskit_ibm_runtime import QiskitRuntimeService, SamplerV2\n\ndef build(backend):\n    circuit = QuantumCircuit(2)\n    circuit.h(0); circuit.cx(0, 1); circuit.measure_all()\n    return generate_preset_pass_manager(backend=backend, optimization_level=1,\n                                        seed_transpiler=17).run(circuit)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument(\"--submit\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"Explicitly submit a paid/allocated hardware job\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--backend\", help=\"Exact accessible hardware backend name\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    if not args.submit:\n        compiled = build(GenericBackendV2(2, seed=3))\n        print(\"Local compilation only; no account accessed and no job submitted.\")\n        print(compiled.count_ops())\n    else:\n        if not args.backend:\n            parser.error(\"--backend is required with --submit\")\n        service = QiskitRuntimeService()  # Uses a separately configured account.\n        backend = service.backend(args.backend)\n        compiled = build(backend)\n        job = SamplerV2(mode=backend).run([compiled], shots=1024)\n        print(\"Submitted job:\", job.job_id())\n        print(job.result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()) Problem 11.3. What is missing from a claim that hardware “gave 95% success”? Worked solution The task, success definition, shots and uncertainty, device and time, circuit and layout, execution options, and comparison baseline are all needed. Hardware can sometimes act on measurement results before a circuit finishes. We next program that feedback explicitly. Sources and further study: IBM, Runtime SamplerV2 ."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-1", "text": "In the companion’s teleportation, Alice obtains \\(c_0=1\\) and \\(c_1=0\\). Which state does Bob hold before correction, and which correction does the program apply? Bob holds \\(Z\\ket\\psi\\) (branch operator \\(X^0Z^1\\)). The \\(X\\) condition on \\(c_1\\) does not fire, the \\(Z\\) condition on \\(c_0\\) does, and \\(Z\\cdot Z\\ket\\psi=\\ket\\psi\\). The verification measurement on Bob then gives \\(0\\)."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-2", "text": "Prepare a Bell pair and reset qubit 0. Using DensityMatrix.evolve, find the joint state afterwards. What happened to qubit 1’s reduced state and to the correlation? The joint state becomes \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I/2\\): qubit 0 is \\(\\ket0\\), qubit 1 is maximally mixed, and the correlation is gone. Qubit 1’s reduced state is unchanged, as no-signalling requires, but the reset destroyed the entanglement. Reset is a physical, irreversible operation, not a reassignment of a variable."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-3", "text": "The companion’s counts are written c2 c1 c0. Decode the key 010: which corrections were applied on those shots, and did the verification succeed? c2 \\(=0\\), c1 \\(=1\\), c0 \\(=0\\). Alice’s records called for an X correction and no Z correction, and the verification bit c2 \\(=0\\) shows that Bob’s corrected state matched the input. Decoding must use the register order; reading the key left to right as c0, c1, c2 would misidentify both the branch and the result."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-4", "text": "Swap the correction rules in the teleportation program, so that X is conditioned on c0 and Z on c1. Run it in Aer and inspect the verification bit for each branch. Which branches fail, and why does one branch still pass? Branches \\(c_1c_0=00\\) and \\(11\\) still verify (c2 \\(=0\\)): branch \\(00\\) needs no correction, and in branch \\(11\\) the swapped rules apply both corrections in the opposite order, which differs only by a global sign. Branches \\(01\\) and \\(10\\) receive the wrong Pauli and fail most of the time. A test that only looked at the aggregate success fraction could be fooled; checking every branch separately exposes the bug immediately."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-5", "text": "Rewrite teleportation with deferred measurement: replace the classically conditioned X and Z by cx(1, 2) and cz(0, 2) applied before any measurement. Verify with Statevector that, after the inverse test preparation on Bob’s qubit, Bob always reads \\(0\\). What practical difference remains between the two versions? With cx(1, 2) and cz(0, 2) in place of the conditional corrections, Bob’s qubit ends in \\(\\ket\\psi\\) for every branch, and after the inverse preparation it reads \\(0\\) with probability \\(1\\); the circuit no longer needs mid-circuit measurement or feedback. The difference is physical: the deferred version needs quantum two-qubit gates between Alice’s and Bob’s qubits, so it cannot be used at a distance, while the dynamic version needs only classical communication, at the cost of measurement and feedback latency."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-6", "text": "Reuse one qubit for two independent random bits: apply H, measure into c0, reset, apply H again, and measure into c1. Run it in Aer and check that the four outcomes are about equally frequent. Is the reset necessary here? Would it be if the second H were replaced by ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))? The four outcomes occur about \\(1000\\) times each in \\(4000\\) shots: two independent fair bits from one qubit. In this particular example the reset happens not to matter, because H maps both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to states with equal probabilities. With ry(\\(\\pi/3\\)) as the second step it would matter: from \\(\\ket0\\) the second bit is \\(1\\) with probability \\(\\frac14\\), but from \\(\\ket1\\) with probability \\(\\frac34\\), so without reset the second bit would depend on the first. Reset returns the reused qubit to the known state that the next step assumes; mid-circuit measurement and reset let a small device act like a larger one over time."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-7", "text": "Implement active reset with feedback: prepare \\(\\ket+\\), measure into c0, and apply X if c0 is \\(1\\); then measure again into c1. Show that c1 is always \\(0\\). How does this differ from the reset instruction, and what can make it fail on hardware? The second measurement always gives \\(0\\) in simulation: the qubit is returned to \\(\\ket0\\) whatever the first outcome. This is how reset is often implemented physically. On hardware it fails when the first measurement reports the wrong value (the correction is then applied or skipped wrongly), when the qubit relaxes or is excited during the feedback delay, or when the measurement leaves it outside the qubit subspace. The reset instruction hides these details, but they still set its error rate."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student says: “I don’t need feedforward for teleportation. I can run the circuit without corrections and afterwards keep only the shots where Alice measured 00.” Compare the fraction of usable shots in the two approaches, and explain why the postselected version is not teleportation. Postselection keeps only the \\(25\\%\\) of shots in branch \\(00\\), and it decides after the run which shots to keep: in any single run Bob’s qubit was wrong three times out of four, and he could not have used it for anything before the data were sorted. Feedforward corrects every branch during the run, so all shots deliver the state and Bob’s qubit is usable immediately. Postselection estimates what teleportation would give; it does not perform the task."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-9", "text": "In dynamic teleportation, Bob’s qubit must wait for Alice’s measurement (\\(1\\) μs) and the classical decision (\\(0.5\\) μs). (a) With \\(T_2=100\\) μs, estimate the coherence loss during the wait. (b) Compare with the deferred version, which instead adds two two-qubit gates with error \\(5\\times10^{-3}\\) each. (c) Which is better here, and what changes the answer? (a) \\(1-e^{-1.5/100}\\approx1.5\\times10^{-2}\\). (b) About \\(10^{-2}\\) from the two extra gates, if the qubits can interact at all. (c) Here the deferred version is slightly better, but only when quantum gates between the parties are possible; with faster feedback or longer \\(T_2\\), the dynamic version wins, and at a distance it is the only option. Latency is a resource to budget, like gate error."}, {"title": "Dynamic Circuits — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#exercise-10", "text": "Validate teleportation more strongly by teleporting half of an entangled pair. Let qubit 3 be a reference entangled with the input qubit 0 in a Bell state. After teleportation and corrections, undo the Bell preparation on the reference and Bob’s qubit and measure both. What outcome indicates success, and why is this test stronger than teleporting one known state? After corrections, cx(3, 2) and h(3) map the reference–Bob Bell pair back to \\(\\ket{00}\\), so both final measurements give \\(0\\) on every shot. Preserving entanglement with an untouched reference tests the channel on all input states at once (it is equivalent to checking the full process), whereas one known test state checks only one input. Any error that would corrupt some inputs shows up as nonzero outcomes here."}, {"title": "Mid-Circuit Operations", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-1", "text": "12.1 Mid-Circuit Operations Teleportation is a demanding program test because every measurement branch must recover the same input state. Averaging only the easiest branch would hide a broken correction rule. 12.1.1 Measurement and reset A mid-circuit measurement writes a classical bit while other quantum wires remain available. Reset prepares a wire in zero but can destroy its previous correlations. These are physical operations, not Python variable assignments. The reference StatevectorSampler is intended for suitable final-measurement workloads; the companion uses Aer for measurement-conditioned circuit execution. 12.1.2 Classical conditions and feedback The with circuit.if_test((bit,1)) context appends an operation conditioned on a classical record. A host-language if cannot inspect the result of an unexecuted circuit. The condition refers to a classical bit or register, so use the exact object and encoding intended by the measurement map. Problem 12.1. Why apply Z controlled by c0 rather than c1? Worked solution The H measurement on the original input determines the phase correction. The Bell-half measurement determines the bit-flip correction. Swapping these records fails on general input states."}, {"title": "Conditional Programs", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-2", "text": "12.2 Conditional Programs 12.2.1 Teleportation The example places the input on q0, Alice’s Bell half on q1, and Bob’s on q2. After CX(0,1) and H(0), Alice measures q0 into c0 and q1 into c1. Bob applies X when c1 is one and then Z when c0 is one. These corrections invert the branch operator X^c1 Z^c0 up to an irrelevant branch phase. 12.2.2 Iterative procedures For a known test input prepared by Ry(0.7) then Rz(0.4), apply its inverse on Bob after correction. Measuring Bob should then give zero in every branch. This verifies a state with nontrivial amplitudes and relative phase. Repeat with other preparations or an entangled reference for stronger channel validation; one test state is not complete process tomography. Problem 12.2. Does Bob receive the state before Alice communicates her outcomes? Worked solution Without the records his state is averaged over the four Pauli branches. The corrections require those classical bits, so the protocol does not signal instantly. Teleportation with classical feedforward. Alice’s top measurement gives m and the middle gives n; Bob applies X^n then Z^m using the communicated records. No quantum control from the measured wires is implied."}, {"title": "Execution Constraints", "chapter": "Dynamic Circuits", "url": "12-dynamic-circuits.html#section-12-3", "text": "12.3 Execution Constraints 12.3.1 Simulator and backend capabilities A simulator accepting if_test does not prove a selected device supports the same dynamic circuit. Check backend capabilities, supported conditions, reset behavior, and timing constraints before submission. The companion runs locally and does not imply hardware availability. A controller’s limited classical expressions may require rewriting a high-level condition. 12.3.2 Latency, branches, and correctness Feedback can save qubits or coherent depth, but adds measurement and classical latency. In the counts below, bit strings are c2 c1 c0; c2 is the final verification result and must be zero. The lower two bits vary because Alice’s Bell measurement is random. We assert correctness of every observed branch, not only the aggregate fraction of convenient outcomes. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_12.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 12.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit_aer import AerSimulator\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\n\n# q0=input, q1=Alice's half, q2=Bob's half; c0,c1 are correction records.\nqc = QuantumCircuit(3, 3)\nqc.ry(0.7, 0); qc.rz(0.4, 0)\nqc.h(1); qc.cx(1, 2)\nqc.cx(0, 1); qc.h(0)\nqc.measure(0, 0); qc.measure(1, 1)\nwith qc.if_test((qc.clbits[1], 1)):\n    qc.x(2)\nwith qc.if_test((qc.clbits[0], 1)):\n    qc.z(2)\n# Inverse of the known test preparation verifies the recovered state.\nqc.rz(-0.4, 2); qc.ry(-0.7, 2)\nqc.measure(2, 2)\nsim = AerSimulator(seed_simulator=23, max_parallel_threads=1)\ncounts = sim.run(transpile(qc, sim), shots=1024).result().get_counts()\nassert all(key[0] == \"0\" for key in counts)\nprint(\"c2 c1 c0:\", counts) Problem 12.3. Why test a nontrivial phase as well as real amplitudes? Worked solution A missing Z correction may be invisible on a basis state. A phase-sensitive input exposes that error. Correct dynamic logic can still fail under noise. The next chapter separates noise models from sampling and approximation. Sources and further study: IBM, classical feedforward ."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-1", "text": "With the depolarizing convention \\(\\mathcal E(\\rho)=(1-p)\\rho+pI/2\\) attached to X, predict \\(P(1)\\) after X on \\(\\ket0\\) for \\(p=0.3\\), and check it with a noisy Aer simulation. \\(P(1)=0.7+0.15=0.85\\), so the bit is wrong with probability \\(0.15=p/2\\). The simulation with \\(20\\,000\\) shots agrees to within sampling error. Reporting “\\(30\\%\\) depolarizing” without its convention invites a factor-of-two misunderstanding of the error rate."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-2", "text": "A readout error reports 1 with probability \\(0.03\\) for a true \\(0\\) and reports 0 with probability \\(0.05\\) for a true \\(1\\). Predict the reported \\(P(1)\\) for an idle qubit and for X\\(\\ket0\\), and check with Aer’s ReadoutError. Idle qubit: reported \\(P(1)=0.03\\). After X: reported \\(P(1)=0.95\\). Readout errors change only the classical label, and their two directions can differ, so they must be calibrated with both preparations."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-3", "text": "Inverse correction of a symmetric readout error multiplies the variance of the estimated \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) by \\(1/(1-2e)^2\\). Evaluate this factor for \\(e=0.05\\) and \\(e=0.3\\). How many more shots are needed to keep the same precision? \\(e=0.05\\): factor \\(1/0.81\\approx1.23\\), so about \\(23\\%\\) more shots. \\(e=0.3\\): factor \\(1/0.16=6.25\\), more than six times the shots. Mitigation trades bias for variance, and the cost grows rapidly as the error approaches \\(\\frac12\\)."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-4", "text": "A noise model contains both a depolarizing error (\\(p=0.1\\)) on X and the asymmetric readout error above. (a) Predict \\(P(1)\\) for an idle qubit and after X. (b) Explain how running both experiments separates gate error from readout error. (c) Check the predictions with Aer. (a) Idle: only readout acts, so \\(P(1)=0.03\\). After X: the true \\(P(1)\\) is \\(0.95\\), and readout reports \\(0.03\\times0.05+0.95\\times0.95\\approx0.904\\). (b) The idle experiment measures readout error alone (no X applied); the X experiment combines both. With readout calibrated from idle and from a separately trusted preparation, the extra loss in the X experiment is attributed to the gate. (c) The simulation gives about \\(0.031\\) and \\(0.902\\)."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-5", "text": "Mitigate the readout error for the state ry(\\(\\pi/3\\))\\(\\ket0\\), whose ideal \\(P(1)\\) is \\(0.25\\). (a) Calibrate the assignment matrix from idle and X runs. (b) Measure the noisy distribution of the test state. (c) Solve the linear system for the corrected distribution and compare with \\(0.25\\). The calibration gives \\(A\\approx\\begin{pmatrix}0.97&0.05\\\\0.03&0.95\\end{pmatrix}\\). The noisy test distribution reports \\(P(1)\\approx0.26\\). Solving \\(Ap=p_{\\rm meas}\\) returns \\(P(1)\\approx0.251\\), close to the ideal \\(0.25\\). The correction assumes that readout errors do not depend on what happened before measurement and that the calibration still describes the device; it removes readout bias but does nothing about gate errors."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-6", "text": "A noise model attaches a strong depolarizing error to the instruction named h. A circuit applies H twice and measures. (a) Run it with the simulator’s default transpilation and with transpilation to the basis rz, sx, x. (b) Explain the different results. (c) What should be checked whenever a noise model is used? (a) With h preserved, the two noisy Hadamards give \\(P(1)\\approx0.37\\); after transpiling to rz, sx, x, the result is \\(P(1)=0\\): no noise at all. (b) The error is attached to the name h, and in the second circuit every H was rewritten as RZ and SX gates, so the model never fires. (c) Inspect the compiled circuit that is actually simulated and confirm that every intended noisy instruction appears with the name used in the model; otherwise a “noisy” simulation can silently be ideal."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-7", "text": "Zero-noise extrapolation: each X carries depolarizing error \\(p=0.05\\), so \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) after one X is \\(-(0.95)\\). Amplify the noise by inserting pairs of X gates (logically the identity) to get noise scales \\(\\lambda=1,3,5\\). (a) Predict \\(\\langle Z\\rangle(\\lambda)\\). (b) Estimate the zero-noise value from \\(\\lambda=1,3\\) with the Richardson formula \\((3E_1-E_3)/2\\). (c) Check with Aer and state the assumptions. (a) \\(E(\\lambda)=-(0.95)^\\lambda\\): \\(-0.950\\), \\(-0.857\\), \\(-0.774\\). (b) \\((3(-0.950)-(-0.857))/2\\approx-0.996\\), close to the ideal \\(-1\\). (c) Sampling with \\(2\\times10^5\\) shots reproduces these values. The method assumes the inserted gates scale the noise by exactly \\(\\lambda\\) and that \\(E(\\lambda)\\) is close to linear over the range used; the extrapolated value also has larger statistical error than the raw measurements."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student calibrates a symmetric readout flip rate of \\(e=0.10\\) and divides every measured \\(\\langle Z\\rangle\\) by \\(1-2e\\). By the time the experiment runs, the true rate has drifted to \\(0.12\\). (a) What fraction of the true value does the “mitigated” result report? (b) Why does collecting more shots not reveal the problem? (a) The measured mean is \\(0.76\\langle Z\\rangle\\), and dividing by \\(0.80\\) gives \\(0.95\\langle Z\\rangle\\): a \\(5\\%\\) bias remains after “mitigation.” (b) More shots shrink the statistical error around the biased value, making a wrong answer look more precise. Mitigation is only as good as its model and calibration; recalibrating close in time, interleaving calibration circuits, and reporting calibration uncertainty are part of the method."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-9", "text": "Full readout mitigation on \\(n\\) qubits calibrates a \\(2^n\\times2^n\\) assignment matrix. (a) How many calibration circuits and matrix entries does this need for \\(n=5\\) and \\(n=20\\)? (b) If readout errors are independent per qubit, how does a tensor-product model reduce the cost? (c) What error does that assumption risk? (a) \\(n=5\\): \\(32\\) calibration circuits and \\(1024\\) entries. \\(n=20\\): about \\(10^6\\) circuits and \\(10^{12}\\) entries, infeasible. (b) With independent errors the matrix is a tensor product of \\(n\\) one-qubit matrices: two calibration circuits per qubit (or two in total, run in parallel) and \\(4n\\) numbers. (c) Correlated readout errors, such as crosstalk between neighbouring readout resonators, are ignored and leave residual bias. Scalable mitigation trades completeness for assumptions that must themselves be tested."}, {"title": "Noise and Reliability — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#exercise-10", "text": "An idle qubit suffers quasi-static dephasing that a pair of X gates (an echo) would refocus, reducing the idle error from \\(\\epsilon_{\\rm idle}\\) to nearly zero, but each X adds error \\(5\\times10^{-4}\\). (a) For which \\(\\epsilon_{\\rm idle}\\) does adding the echo help? (b) What if the dephasing noise changes quickly during the idle window? (c) What should a program report when it adds suppression gates? (a) The echo helps when \\(\\epsilon_{\\rm idle}\\) exceeds about \\(10^{-3}\\), the error added by its two gates. (b) If the noise varies during the window, the echo refocuses only part of it, and the break-even idle error is larger; for very fast noise the gates only add error. (c) A baseline without suppression, the added gate count and duration, and measured results for both, so that the benefit is demonstrated rather than assumed."}, {"title": "Noise Models", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-1", "text": "13.1 Noise Models An X gate should produce one from zero, yet a noisy device sometimes reports zero. Before attempting a correction, distinguish an erroneous gate from an erroneous readout and from finite sampling fluctuations. 13.1.1 Gate and readout errors A gate error changes the quantum state before measurement; a readout error changes the reported classical label. The example attaches a depolarizing channel to X: E(ρ)=0.9ρ+0.1I/2. On the intended one state, the measured probability of one is 0.95. The parameter 0.1 is not itself the probability of a wrong reported bit under this convention. 13.1.2 Constructing noisy simulations NoiseModel assigns errors to instruction names and qubits. If transpilation removes or renames an instruction, a model attached to its old name may no longer act where intended. Inspect the compiled circuit. The companion uses optimization_level=0 and an explicit X experiment so the attachment remains clear. A chosen model is a hypothesis about errors, not a calibrated device by itself. Problem 13.1. For depolarization strength 0.2 after X, predict P(1). Worked solution The state is 0.8|1〉〈1|+0.2I/2, so P(1)=0.9."}, {"title": "Reliability Techniques", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-2", "text": "13.2 Reliability Techniques 13.2.1 Error suppression Suppression attempts to reduce errors during execution: better layouts, shorter circuits, or properly scheduled decoupling can help under appropriate noise assumptions. Adding gates can also make errors worse. Compare a controlled baseline and count the added duration and operations rather than assuming that a named technique always helps. 13.2.2 Error mitigation and its assumptions Mitigation estimates an ideal quantity from noisy data, often by calibration, inversion, or extrapolation. For symmetric readout flip probability e, the observed Z mean is (1−2e) times the true mean; dividing by that factor corrects the mean if the model is right. Its variance grows by 1/(1−2e)^2, diverging as e approaches one half. Calibration uncertainty and drift must also be included. Problem 13.2. At readout flip rate e=0.1, how does inverse correction scale variance? Worked solution It multiplies variance by 1/0.8²=1.5625, even if e is known exactly."}, {"title": "Interpreting Discrepancies", "chapter": "Noise and Reliability", "url": "13-noise-and-reliability.html#section-13-3", "text": "13.3 Interpreting Discrepancies 13.3.1 Noise, approximation, and sampling A discrepancy can come from a wrong program, an approximate circuit, random shots, or a misspecified noise model. Check them in that order with increasingly realistic experiments. More shots reduce random fluctuation but do not fix a biased model. Comparing an exact simulator with noisy hardware without those distinctions is not a diagnosis. 13.3.2 Bias, uncertainty, and reporting limits The companion runs 8192 simulated shots and checks the frequency against 0.95 with a conservative tolerance. It does not claim that depolarization describes every device or that the same tolerance is a confidence interval for all experiments. Report the model, parameter convention, compiled gate attachment, seed, and shot count with the output. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_13.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 13.\"\"\"\nfrom qiskit_aer import AerSimulator\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, transpile\nfrom qiskit_aer.noise import NoiseModel, depolarizing_error\n\nqc = QuantumCircuit(1)\nqc.x(0); qc.measure_all()\nnoise = NoiseModel()\nnoise.add_all_qubit_quantum_error(depolarizing_error(0.1, 1), [\"x\"])\nsim = AerSimulator(noise_model=noise, seed_simulator=31, max_parallel_threads=1)\ncounts = sim.run(transpile(qc, sim, optimization_level=0), shots=8192).result().get_counts()\np1 = counts.get(\"1\", 0) / 8192\n# E(rho) = .9 rho + .1 I/2, so expected P(1) = .95.\nassert abs(p1 - 0.95) < 0.03\nprint(\"Simulated P(1):\", p1, \"analytic prediction: 0.95\") Problem 13.3. Can error mitigation certify a fault-tolerant computation? Worked solution No. Mitigation depends on a noise model and statistical processing. It does not supply an error-correcting logical computation or a general guarantee against arbitrary errors. The next program uses repeated estimates to choose parameters, making the statistical budget part of the optimization loop. Sources and further study: IBM, noise model construction ."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the trial state with \\(E(\\theta)=-1-\\sin\\theta\\), compute the energy and the parameter-shift gradient \\(\\frac12[E(\\theta+\\pi/2)-E(\\theta-\\pi/2)]\\) at \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), using the Estimator. Compare with \\(-\\cos\\theta\\). \\(E(\\pi/6)=-1.5\\) and the parameter-shift gradient is \\(-0.866=-\\cos(\\pi/6)\\), exact for this single-rotation circuit. The rule needs two energy evaluations per parameter, which on hardware means two separately estimated energies, each with shot noise."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-2", "text": "The companion’s grid has \\(101\\) points on \\([0,\\pi]\\). Estimate the worst-case energy error at the grid minimum, using \\(E\\approx-2+\\frac12(\\theta-\\pi/2)^2\\) near the optimum. Is the grid fine enough here? The spacing is about \\(0.031\\), so the nearest grid point is at most \\(0.016\\) from \\(\\pi/2\\), giving an energy error of at most \\(\\frac12(0.016)^2\\approx1.2\\times10^{-4}\\). Here \\(\\pi/2\\) is actually a grid point (index \\(50\\)), so the error is zero. Energy is quadratic near a minimum, so modest grids give accurate energies, though less accurate parameters."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-3", "text": "A gradient-based optimization uses \\(10\\) parameters, the parameter-shift rule, and \\(50\\) iterations, each energy estimated with \\(10^4\\) shots. How many energy evaluations and shots does it need, ignoring line searches and restarts? \\(2\\times10\\times50=1000\\) energy evaluations, or \\(10^7\\) shots, plus any evaluations of the energy itself for stopping decisions. The classical loop looks short, but the quantum cost scales with parameters, iterations, and precision, and must be budgeted before running on hardware."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-4", "text": "Implement gradient descent with the parameter-shift rule, starting at \\(\\theta=0.2\\), for \\(20\\) steps. Compare learning rates \\(\\eta=0.01\\), \\(1.0\\), and \\(2.5\\). Which reaches the minimum, and why do the others fail? With \\(\\eta=1\\), the iteration reaches \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\) and \\(E=-2\\). With \\(\\eta=0.01\\) it barely moves (to about \\(0.39\\)) in \\(20\\) steps. With \\(\\eta=2.5\\) it overshoots: near the minimum the error is multiplied by about \\(1-\\eta=-1.5\\) each step, so it oscillates and grows instead of converging. Step size must match the landscape’s curvature; the analytic curve lets the program be tested before noise complicates the picture."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-5", "text": "Estimate the energy at \\(\\theta=\\pi/3\\) from samples instead of exactly: measure ZZ directly and XX after Hadamards, with \\(4096\\) shots each. (a) What are the exact energy and the single-shot variances? (b) What standard error should the estimate have? (c) Check the sampled estimate. (a) \\(E=-1-\\sin\\frac\\pi3\\approx-1.866\\). The ZZ term has zero variance on this family, and the XX term has variance \\(1-0.75=0.25\\). (b) The standard error is about \\(\\sqrt{0.25/4096}\\approx0.008\\), all from the XX term. (c) The sampled estimate lands within a few hundredths of \\(-1.866\\). A hardware optimizer sees energies with this kind of noise, which affects gradients and stopping rules."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-6", "text": "Change the Hamiltonian to \\(H=ZZ-XX\\) while keeping the same ansatz. (a) Evaluate the ansatz energy over a full grid \\(\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi]\\) and find its minimum. (b) Compute the true ground energy by diagonalizing \\(H\\). (c) Explain the gap. (a) The ansatz energy is \\(1-\\sin\\theta\\), with minimum \\(0\\). (b) The true ground energy is \\(-2\\), reached by \\((\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). (c) The ansatz only produces states with equal bits (\\(ZZ=+1\\)), so it cannot reach the ground state, which has unequal bits. No optimizer can fix an ansatz that excludes the answer; checking against exact diagonalization on small instances exposes the expressiveness gap."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-7", "text": "Implement depth-1 QAOA for MaxCut on one edge: H on both qubits, the cost evolution as rzz(\\(-\\gamma\\)), and the mixer as rx(\\(2\\beta\\)) on each qubit. Use the observable \\(C=\\frac12(II-ZZ)\\). Evaluate \\(\\langle C\\rangle\\) at several \\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) and compare with \\(F=\\frac12+\\frac12\\sin4\\beta\\sin\\gamma\\). Watch the parameter order. Because the circuit’s parameters are ordered beta, gamma, each value row must be written as \\([\\beta,\\gamma]\\). With that order, the Estimator reproduces \\(F\\) exactly: \\(1\\) at \\((\\gamma,\\beta)=(\\pi/2,\\pi/8)\\), \\(0.854\\) at \\((\\pi/2,\\pi/16)\\) and at \\((\\pi/4,\\pi/8)\\), and \\(0\\) at \\((\\pi/2,3\\pi/8)\\). Supplying \\([\\gamma,\\beta]\\) instead gives \\(0.5\\) everywhere for these points, a silent error that the analytic comparison catches."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-8", "text": "A noisy hardware run reports \\(E=-2.03\\pm0.02\\) for \\(H=-ZZ-XX\\), whose true ground energy is \\(-2\\). A student concludes that the optimizer found “a better solution than the ground state.” Explain what the result actually indicates. No state has energy below \\(-2\\), so the reading is an estimation artifact: \\(-2.03\\) is \\(1.5\\) standard errors below the bound, consistent with statistical fluctuation, or with bias from noise or mitigation. Selecting the lowest of many noisy evaluations also biases the reported minimum downward. The right response is to re-estimate the energy at the chosen parameters with fresh shots and report it with its uncertainty, not to accept the lowest value seen."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-9", "text": "Compare gradient estimators under shot noise. Each energy estimate has standard error \\(\\sigma=0.01\\). (a) The parameter-shift rule uses shifts of \\(\\pm\\pi/2\\). Find the standard error of its gradient. (b) A central finite difference uses \\(\\pm h\\) with \\(h=0.01\\). Find its standard error. (c) Why is a small \\(h\\) attractive in exact arithmetic but harmful with shots? (a) \\(\\sigma\\sqrt2/2\\approx0.007\\). (b) \\(\\sigma\\sqrt2/(2h)\\approx0.71\\), a hundred times larger than the gradient’s typical size. (c) Small \\(h\\) reduces the truncation error of a finite difference, but it divides the noise by \\(h\\). Parameter shift is exact for Pauli-rotation parameters and uses large shifts, so it does not amplify noise. Gradient methods must be chosen for noisy evaluations, not exact ones."}, {"title": "Hybrid Quantum Programs — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#exercise-10", "text": "An optimization is run from \\(20\\) random initial points and the best result is reported. Each run uses \\(500\\) energy evaluations of \\(10^4\\) shots. (a) What is the total quantum cost? (b) If \\(15\\) runs converge to a local minimum and \\(5\\) to the global one, what should the report include? (c) Why does reporting only the best run misrepresent the method? (a) \\(20\\times500\\times10^4=10^8\\) shots. (b) The success rate (\\(5/20\\)), the distribution of final energies, the evaluation budget per run, the seeds, and the total cost. (c) The best run alone hides the \\(75\\%\\) failure rate and the twentyfold cost of finding it; a different method that succeeds from every start may be better even if its best result is identical."}, {"title": "Parameterized Workflows", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-1", "text": "14.1 Parameterized Workflows A variational program repeatedly prepares a parameterized state and asks for its energy. Start with an objective whose entire curve is known, so the optimizer cannot conceal an implementation mistake. 14.1.1 Parameter binding and objectives Prepare cos(θ/2)|00〉+sin(θ/2)|11〉 using Ry on qubit zero followed by CNOT. For H=−ZZ−XX, the energy is −1−sin θ. The minimum −2 occurs at θ=π/2. This analytic curve is the oracle for testing the program; the numerical search should reproduce it before any larger problem is attempted. 14.1.2 Repeated estimation Keep the circuit symbolic and bind parameters through the primitive’s input array. Each evaluation has a cost, even if the host loop is short. The companion evaluates a grid in one local Estimator workload. Exact local expectations remove shot noise from this first debugging stage; a hardware experiment would need a measurement allocation and confidence analysis. Problem 14.1. Why is the energy at θ=0 equal to −1? Worked solution The state is 00, so ZZ has expectation one and XX has expectation zero. A one-parameter entangled trial state: cos(θ/2)|00⟩+sin(θ/2)|11⟩. Measure ZZ directly or rotate both wires with H before measuring XX."}, {"title": "Optimization Loops", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-2", "text": "14.2 Optimization Loops 14.2.1 Gradients and classical updates For a single Pauli-generated rotation, the derivative is half the difference of energies at θ±π/2. A gradient step subtracts a learning-rate multiple of that derivative. A grid search is simpler for the one-parameter example and gives a reproducible baseline. Large parameter spaces require more careful optimization, but complicated optimizers do not fix a wrong circuit or objective. 14.2.2 Stopping criteria and reproducibility Stop according to an energy-improvement and uncertainty criterion, plus an evaluation budget. Fix initial points and random seeds when comparing methods. A noisy objective can have biased gradients and flat regions. Record failed runs and initialization sensitivity; choosing only the best seed after many attempts understates classical and quantum costs. Problem 14.2. Can a measured energy below −2 be accepted as a better solution? Worked solution Not for this exact Hamiltonian. It indicates statistical fluctuation, bias, or a programming error; the true minimum is −2."}, {"title": "Applications", "chapter": "Hybrid Quantum Programs", "url": "14-hybrid-quantum-programs.html#section-14-3", "text": "14.3 Applications 14.3.1 Variational eigensolvers A variational eigensolver is a trial family, observable estimator, and classical parameter search. The variational bound says each exact trial energy is at least the ground energy; it does not guarantee that the family contains the ground state or that optimization finds its best member. Noisy estimates can violate that bound numerically without violating quantum mechanics. 14.3.2 QAOA and performance comparisons QAOA changes the trial family to alternating cost and mixer evolutions. Compare its output distribution with a classical heuristic on the same instances and include training evaluations. The companion’s solved two-qubit energy demonstrates a correct loop interface, not quantum advantage. It verifies the whole sampled parameter grid against the analytic curve and reports the minimum. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_14.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 14.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.circuit import Parameter\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorEstimator\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import SparsePauliOp\n\ntheta = Parameter(\"theta\")\nqc = QuantumCircuit(2)\nqc.ry(theta, 0); qc.cx(0, 1)\nH = SparsePauliOp.from_list([(\"ZZ\", -1), (\"XX\", -1)])\nangles = np.linspace(0, np.pi, 101)\nenergies = StatevectorEstimator().run([(qc, H, angles[:, None])]).result()[0].data.evs\nassert np.allclose(energies, -1 - np.sin(angles))\nbest = int(np.argmin(energies))\nassert np.isclose(energies[best], -2)\nprint(\"Grid minimum:\", angles[best], energies[best]) Problem 14.3. Why keep a grid baseline? Worked solution It provides a transparent reference on the small problem and exposes errors in sophisticated optimization machinery. Its cost grows rapidly with parameter dimension, so it is not a general scalable strategy. The next chapter implements operator constructions whose approximation and normalization must be tested as carefully as their code."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-1", "text": "To simulate \\(e^{-i(X+Z)t}\\) for \\(t=2\\) with \\(r=10\\) first-order steps, what angle should each rz and rx receive? Why is it not \\(t/r\\)? Each step needs \\(e^{-iZt/r}\\), and \\(R_z(\\theta)=e^{-i\\theta Z/2}\\), so \\(\\theta=2t/r=0.4\\); likewise for rx. Using \\(t/r\\) would simulate half the intended time. The factor of two comes from the convention of Qiskit’s rotation gates and must be checked against the formula, as the chapter does."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-2", "text": "For the LCU encoding of \\(A=(I+X)/2\\) (ancilla q1, system q0), compute the probability that the ancilla reads \\(0\\) for system inputs \\(\\ket0\\), \\(\\ket+\\), and \\(\\ket-\\). Explain the three values. \\(\\ket0\\): \\(\\|(\\ket0+\\ket1)/2\\|^2=\\frac12\\). \\(\\ket+\\): \\(A\\ket+=\\ket+\\), probability \\(1\\). \\(\\ket-\\): \\(A\\ket-=0\\), probability \\(0\\): the two terms cancel completely. The success probability depends on the input, which is why a block encoding’s cost must be stated for the inputs it will see."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-3", "text": "Rebuild the LCU circuit with the ancilla on qubit 0 and the system on qubit 1. Which rows and columns of the operator now form the selected block? With the ancilla as qubit 0 (the low bit), ancilla \\(=0\\) corresponds to indices \\(0\\) and \\(2\\), so the block is the submatrix with rows and columns \\([0,2]\\), not the top-left \\(2\\times2\\) corner. Extracting the wrong slice would “verify” a different matrix. The ancilla’s position must be part of the encoding’s documented interface."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compare the first-order product formula with the symmetric second-order formula, rx(\\(1/r\\)), rz(\\(2/r\\)), rx(\\(1/r\\)) per step, for \\(H=X+Z\\), \\(t=1\\), and \\(r=10,20,40\\). Compute both errors against the exact unitary and describe how each scales with \\(r\\). First order: about \\(0.070\\), \\(0.035\\), \\(0.0175\\), halving with each doubling of \\(r\\) (error \\(\\propto1/r\\)). Second order: about \\(2.7\\times10^{-3}\\), \\(6.8\\times10^{-4}\\), \\(1.7\\times10^{-4}\\), falling fourfold (error \\(\\propto1/r^2\\)). The symmetric formula costs only slightly more per step, since adjacent half-steps merge, yet is far more accurate."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-5", "text": "On hardware, each rotation has error about \\(10^{-3}\\), so a first-order simulation with \\(r\\) steps (two rotations each) has total error roughly \\(0.7/r+2\\times10^{-3}r\\). (a) Find the \\(r\\) that minimizes it and the minimum. (b) Compare with \\(r=100\\). (c) How does the second-order formula change the picture? (a) \\(r^*=\\sqrt{0.7/0.002}\\approx19\\), giving \\(0.7/19+0.038\\approx0.075\\). (b) At \\(r=100\\): \\(0.007+0.2=0.207\\), almost three times worse. (c) With a second-order error near \\(0.27/r^2\\), the optimum moves to far fewer steps (about \\(r\\approx6\\) to \\(7\\), total near \\(0.03\\)), because each step is more accurate. On noisy hardware the best formula and step count balance algorithmic and gate errors."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-6", "text": "Build an LCU encoding of \\((Z+X)/2=H/\\sqrt2\\): H on the ancilla (qubit 1), apply Z to the system when the ancilla is \\(0\\) and X when it is \\(1\\), then H on the ancilla. (a) Verify the top-left block. (b) Show that the success probability is \\(\\frac12\\) for every input. (c) Why does this make the encoding a candidate for oblivious amplification? (a) The top-left \\(2\\times2\\) block equals \\(H/\\sqrt2\\). (b) Since \\(H\\) is unitary, \\(\\|H\\ket\\psi\\|^2/2=\\frac12\\) for every input. (c) An encoded matrix proportional to a unitary, with input-independent success probability, is exactly the situation in which oblivious amplitude amplification can boost success to certainty without knowing the input, as Quantum Algorithms Chapter 7 develops."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-7", "text": "For the scalar signal \\(W(x)=xI+i\\sqrt{1-x^2}X\\), verify numerically that (a) the top-left entry of \\(W^3\\) is \\(T_3(x)=4x^3-3x\\), and (b) inserting the phase \\(\\mathrm{diag}(e^{i\\phi},e^{-i\\phi})\\) between two calls gives \\(\\cos\\phi(2x^2-1)+i\\sin\\phi\\). Use \\(x=0.3\\), \\(x=-0.7\\), and \\(\\phi=0.4\\). Both identities hold at the sample points: the three-call response is \\(4x^3-3x\\), and the phased two-call response is \\(\\cos\\phi(2x^2-1)+i\\sin\\phi\\). Point checks catch convention errors (sign of \\(i\\), order of phases, which entry is selected) before a phase list from a synthesis tool is trusted. They do not prove the bound on a whole interval, which must come from the construction."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student applies the LCU circuit for \\(A=(I+X)/2\\) twice in a row, on the same ancilla and without measuring in between, expecting the selected block to be \\(A^2\\). Compute the actual block and explain the discrepancy. The circuit \\(U\\) is its own inverse, so \\(U^2=I\\) and the selected block is the identity, not \\(A^2=(I+X)/2\\). Between the two applications, the amplitude that left the ancilla-\\(0\\) branch returns and interferes. Multiplying encoded blocks requires fresh ancillas (or measurement and postselection between uses), and polynomial constructions insert reflections between calls; repetition alone does not compose blocks."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-9", "text": "A block encoding of \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(1,0.1)\\) with \\(\\alpha=1\\) is applied to the input \\(\\ket1\\). (a) Find the success probability. (b) Estimate the expected number of repetitions with repeat-until-success, and the number of encoding calls with amplitude amplification. (c) What does this imply about reporting the cost of an encoded operation? (a) \\(0.1^2=0.01\\). (b) Repeat-until-success needs about \\(100\\) attempts on average; amplitude amplification about \\(\\frac\\pi4\\cdot10\\approx8\\) rounds, each using the encoding and its inverse, so roughly \\(16\\) calls. (c) The cost depends on the input through \\(\\|A\\psi\\|\\), and on \\(\\alpha\\). A single “one call” figure for the encoding hides a factor that can reach \\(\\alpha^2/\\|A\\psi\\|^2\\), so reports must state the success probability for the relevant inputs and how it is handled."}, {"title": "Advanced Algorithm Construction — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#exercise-10", "text": "A polynomial transformation is promised to act on signals \\(x\\in[-1,1]\\), which holds when the subnormalization satisfies \\(\\alpha\\ge\\|A\\|\\). Suppose \\(\\alpha\\) is underestimated, so that some eigenvalue gives \\(x=1.1\\). (a) Can \\(W(x)=xI+i\\sqrt{1-x^2}X\\) be formed as a unitary? (b) Evaluate \\(T_3(1.1)\\). (c) What does this say about validating \\(\\alpha\\)? (a) No: \\(\\sqrt{1-1.21}\\) is imaginary, the matrix is not unitary, and no circuit implements it; a block of a unitary cannot have norm above \\(1\\). (b) \\(T_3(1.1)=5.324-3.3\\approx2.02\\), outside \\([-1,1]\\), so even the target response would be impossible. (c) The promise \\(\\alpha\\ge\\|A\\|\\) must be verified (from a bound on the norm, or from the construction of the encoding), not assumed. Polynomials that are bounded on \\([-1,1]\\) grow rapidly outside it, so a small underestimate can make an algorithm silently wrong."}, {"title": "Hamiltonian Simulation", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-1", "text": "15.1 Hamiltonian Simulation Advanced circuit boxes often hide two separate issues: how accurately they implement a target transformation, and how often a selected auxiliary outcome occurs. We will expose both with small matrices. 15.1.1 Product-formula circuits For H=X+Z and time one, alternate Rz(2/r) then Rx(2/r), repeated r times. Since Rz(θ)=exp(−iθZ/2), this realizes (exp(−iX/r)exp(−iZ/r))^r. Compare its operator with exp(−i(X+Z)), computed classically only for this small verification instance. The gate order is chosen deliberately to match the formula. 15.1.2 Time and approximation error The first-order norm bound is at most 1/r for this example. The companion checks r=10,20,40 against that bound. It reports approximation error separately from sampling and hardware noise. Increasing r reduces the formula error but adds gates, so on hardware the best r can be finite rather than as large as possible. Problem 15.1. Why is the rotation angle 2/r rather than 1/r? Worked solution Qiskit’s Pauli rotation convention has a factor one half in the exponential. Using 1/r would simulate half the intended term strength."}, {"title": "Operator Embeddings", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-2", "text": "15.2 Operator Embeddings 15.2.1 Ancillas and normalization A block encoding of A/α places that transformation in the zero-ancilla block of a unitary. The scale α and ancilla count are part of the interface. Successful projection produces A|ψ〉/α before normalization. A matrix entry check verifies the encoding, while a state-specific probability check tells how expensive selection will be. 15.2.2 Linear combinations of unitaries For A=(I+X)/2, use an auxiliary H, controlled-X, then H. In the companion, auxiliary q1 is the high bit, so the selected block is the top-left two-by-two block. Reversing the ancilla and system indices changes which matrix slice is correct. Randomly choosing I or X would not implement this coherent sum. Problem 15.2. What is the success probability of the LCU example on system zero? Worked solution The selected vector is (|0〉+|1〉)/2, with squared norm one half. The normalized conditional state is plus. An encoding of (I+X)/2. Select ancilla outcome zero to obtain the desired sum on the system; success depends on the input state."}, {"title": "Polynomial Transformations", "chapter": "Advanced Algorithm Construction", "url": "15-advanced-algorithm-construction.html#section-15-3", "text": "15.3 Polynomial Transformations 15.3.1 Phase sequences Signal processing alternates an encoded signal with phase rotations. Fix the sign convention, ordering, selected auxiliary block, and admissible polynomial before importing a phase list. For the scalar signal W(x)=xI+i√(1−x²)X, two calls give response 2x²−1. Repeating a block encoding does not generally square its selected block, because unselected amplitudes can return. 15.3.2 Verification and compilation costs Validate a candidate polynomial at sample points and at its promised interval boundaries, then test the matrix action on small encoded examples. Sampling is not a proof of a uniform approximation bound, which must come from the construction. Charge encoding calls, normalization, success amplification, phase synthesis, and compilation. The companion verifies product formulas and the basic LCU block used by those more advanced constructions. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_15.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 15.\"\"\"\nimport numpy as np\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\n\nX = np.array([[0, 1], [1, 0]])\nZ = np.diag([1, -1])\nerrors = []\nfor steps in (10, 20, 40):\n    qc = QuantumCircuit(1)\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        qc.rz(2/steps, 0)\n        qc.rx(2/steps, 0)\n    error = np.linalg.norm(Operator(qc).data - expm(-1j*(X+Z)), 2)\n    assert error <= 1/steps + 1e-12\n    errors.append(error)\n# An LCU encoding of (I+X)/2: ancilla q1, system q0.\nlcu = QuantumCircuit(2)\nlcu.h(1); lcu.cx(1, 0); lcu.h(1)\nassert np.allclose(Operator(lcu).data[:2, :2], (np.eye(2)+X)/2)\nprint(\"Product-formula errors:\", errors)\n\n# Verify the low-degree signal response independently of a synthesis package.\nfor x in np.linspace(-1, 1, 21):\n    W = x*np.eye(2) + 1j*np.sqrt(max(0, 1-x*x))*X\n    assert np.allclose(W.conj().T @ W, np.eye(2))\n    assert np.isclose((W @ W)[0, 0], 2*x*x-1) Problem 15.3. Why cannot a degree count alone specify total cost? Worked solution Each signal call may contain a large encoding circuit, and success amplification or gate synthesis adds work. The access implementation matters. The final chapter packages these habits into a reproducible project rather than an isolated successful run."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-1", "text": "For the capstone’s Bernstein–Vazirani construction, which input qubits receive CNOTs to the target for the secret 0110? What should the input register read? Reading 0110 from the right, bits \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) are \\(1\\), so qubits 1 and 2 control CNOTs onto the target. The input register then reads 0110 with certainty (in Qiskit’s order, qubit 0 rightmost), and the full measured string has the target bit on the left."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-2", "text": "For an \\(n\\)-bit secret with \\(k\\) ones, count the gates of the capstone circuit (before measurement). Check the counts and the depth for the all-ones secret with \\(n=10\\). The circuit has \\(1\\) X, \\(2n+1\\) Hadamards, and \\(k\\) CNOTs. For \\(n=10\\), \\(k=10\\): \\(21\\) H, \\(10\\) CNOTs, depth \\(13\\), since the CNOTs all target the same qubit and run one after another. The query count is one, but the oracle’s gate count and depth grow with the secret, which a resource report must include."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-3", "text": "A project records the Qiskit version but not the versions of Aer, NumPy, or the Python interpreter. Which results could fail to reproduce, and what file in this course records the complete environment? Noisy-simulation results depend on the Aer version; numerical tolerances and random streams can change with NumPy and Python; even ideal sampling with a fixed seed can differ between versions of the primitives. requirements.txt pins the direct dependencies, and requirements-lock.txt records the full resolved environment, while TESTING.md notes the platform. A record should cite all of them."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-4", "text": "Turn the capstone into a tested function. (a) Write bernstein_vazirani(secret) and a check that samples the circuit and verifies the input register for every shot. (b) Apply it to 0000, 1111, and three random six-bit secrets from a seeded generator. (c) Why include the all-zero and all-one secrets? The function passes for all five secrets. The all-zero secret has no CNOTs at all, so it checks that the Hadamard layers alone return \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) and that an empty oracle does not break the construction; the all-one secret exercises every CNOT and the longest sequential chain on the target. Random secrets cover typical cases. Edge cases and random cases together make a stronger test than the single secret in the companion."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-5", "text": "Verify the oracle’s phase contract directly: after the oracle acts on the uniform input with the target in \\(\\ket-\\), the input amplitudes should be \\((-1)^{s\\cdot x}/2^{n/2}\\). Implement the check for the secret 1011 by returning the target to \\(\\ket0\\) and comparing the statevector with the predicted sign pattern. Undoing the target preparation leaves the state \\(\\sum_x(-1)^{s\\cdot x}\\ket x/4\\otimes\\ket0\\). Comparing the first \\(16\\) amplitudes (target \\(0\\)) with \\((-1)^{\\mathrm{popcount}(s\\,\\&\\,x)}/4\\) confirms the phase oracle exactly. This tests the oracle’s promised behavior independently of the final Hadamards, so a failure would point to the oracle rather than to the readout."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-6", "text": "Build an experiment record for the capstone that a reader can verify. Include the problem, secret, shots, seed, and package versions for qiskit, qiskit-aer, and numpy, and add a SHA-256 fingerprint of the QPY-serialized coherent circuit. Why is the fingerprint useful? The record below stores the descriptive fields, the versions, and a hash of the serialized circuit. The fingerprint identifies exactly which circuit produced the results: if someone later regenerates the circuit and the hash differs, something in the construction or the software changed. Storing the QPY file itself lets them load and rerun that exact circuit. (QPY files can depend on the Qiskit version, so the version belongs next to the hash.)"}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-7", "text": "Run the capstone (secret 1011) on a noisy simulator in which every CNOT suffers two-qubit depolarizing error \\(0.02\\). (a) Estimate the fraction of shots whose input register equals the secret. (b) Why is it below \\(1\\), and how would it change for a longer secret with more ones? (c) How should the result be labelled? (a) About \\(0.96\\) with \\(8000\\) shots. (b) Each of the three CNOTs fails with probability about \\(0.02\\), and many failures corrupt the readout, so success is roughly \\(1-3\\times0.02\\times(\\text{fraction harmful})\\). A secret with \\(k\\) ones has \\(k\\) noisy CNOTs, so success falls roughly linearly in \\(k\\) for small errors. (c) As a noisy simulation with a stated, hypothetical noise model, distinct from both ideal simulation and hardware, with the model parameters, seed, and shots recorded."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-8", "text": "A student argues: “The capstone finds a \\(50\\)-bit secret with one query, while a classical algorithm needs \\(50\\), so this project demonstrates a quantum speedup.” List what the project actually demonstrates and what a speedup claim would require. The project demonstrates a correct, reproducible implementation of the algorithm in the query model, where the oracle is a supplied interface. Here the oracle was constructed from the known secret, with one CNOT per one-bit, so reading the circuit reveals the secret and building it costs as many gates as the classical queries it replaces. A speedup claim would need a setting where the oracle is genuinely given as a black box, matched classical and quantum access, end-to-end costs including oracle construction and noise, and measured performance as \\(n\\) grows, not a single success."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-9", "text": "Extend the capstone study to \\(n=50\\). (a) Why is a statevector simulation impossible, and why can Aer’s stabilizer method still run the circuit? (b) Run it for the secret “10” repeated \\(25\\) times and check the result. (c) What does this say about choosing a simulation method in a scaling study? (a) \\(51\\) qubits need \\(2^{51}\\approx2\\times10^{15}\\) amplitudes, about \\(36\\) petabytes. The circuit uses only Clifford gates (X, H, CNOT), which the stabilizer method simulates with polynomial resources. (b) Every shot returns the secret in the input register. (c) The simulator must match the circuit class: stabilizer methods scale for Clifford circuits, tensor networks for low-entanglement ones, statevectors for small general circuits. A scaling study should state which was used, since simulation cost is a property of the method, not evidence about quantum hardware."}, {"title": "Reproducible Quantum Projects — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#exercise-10", "text": "Design the directory layout and test plan for turning the capstone into a reusable project. Name the modules, what each test checks, and how hardware submission is kept from happening by accident. A reasonable layout: a package module with the circuit factory (bernstein_vazirani) and the oracle; an experiment driver script that builds, compiles, runs, and writes a JSON record and QPY file; a tests directory with exact tests (phase contract, statevector output for small \\(n\\), edge-case secrets), seeded sampling tests, a noisy-simulation smoke test, and a serialization round-trip test; and requirements and lock files with a README describing the environment. The driver submits to hardware only with an explicit option and backend name, and importing any module never submits. Continuous testing reruns the suite whenever dependencies change."}, {"title": "Project Structure", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-1", "text": "16.1 Project Structure A project is reproducible when another reader can reconstruct its task, circuit, assumptions, and evidence. A plot without the program and execution record is not enough. 16.1.1 Modules, notebooks, and tests Keep circuit factories, experiment drivers, and tests separate. A notebook can explain an experiment, while a script runs it from a fresh process. Tests should include a small independently solvable case, asymmetric ordering checks, and phase-sensitive inputs. Never make importing a module submit a hardware job. 16.1.2 Dependencies and version control Pin tested dependencies and record the Python version. Keep credentials out of version control. Record source changes so that a result points to the code that produced it. A requirements file captures direct dependencies; a full environment lock and platform note improve exact reproduction. Updating a package requires rerunning relevant examples rather than assuming unchanged behavior. Problem 16.1. Why does the target bit vary in the capstone output? Worked solution It remains in minus, which gives a fair computational-basis result. The useful secret is in the other bits, so the decoder excludes the target bit."}, {"title": "Experiment Records", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-2", "text": "16.2 Experiment Records 16.2.1 Saved circuits and configurations Save a circuit artifact as well as its drawing. QPY preserves Qiskit circuit structure for supported version compatibility, and the companion checks an in-memory save/load round trip. Keep the logical circuit, compiled circuit, layout, parameter values, and seeds when compilation is involved. A diagram alone may omit global phases or parameter metadata. 16.2.2 Results, metadata, and execution costs An experiment record should identify the problem, input size, access assumptions, success criterion, shots, qubits, logical and compiled counts, execution type, package versions, and measured outputs. Exact local simulation, noisy simulation, and hardware must be labeled distinctly. Confidence intervals and error budgets belong beside conclusions, not hidden in raw logs. Problem 16.2. What changes if the secret is all zeros? Worked solution The oracle has no CNOTs and the input Hadamards cancel, so the output secret is all zeros. This is an important edge case."}, {"title": "Capstone Project", "chapter": "Reproducible Quantum Projects", "url": "16-reproducible-quantum-projects.html#section-16-3", "text": "16.3 Capstone Project 16.3.1 Specification and implementation The capstone implements Bernstein–Vazirani for an arbitrary supplied binary secret. It prepares a minus target, uses one CNOT for each one bit in the oracle description, applies Hadamards, and reads the input register. The known secret lets us verify construction; it is not being discovered by a program that lacks access to its own source. The algorithmic query model treats the constructed oracle as the supplied interface. 16.3.2 Evaluation, limitations, and documentation The companion uses secret 1011, checks circuit serialization, samples, and verifies every input-register result. The target is measured too and appears at the left of Qiskit’s full string; bits[1:] extracts the input register. Extend the project across lengths, test zero and all-one secrets, report oracle gate growth, and compare with the classical query baseline under the same oracle contract. The result is a reproducible implementation study, not a hardware speedup claim. The complete companion below can be downloaded as chapter_16.py . Run it from a terminal using the course environment. Assertions check the stated behavior; printed counts are samples, not universal expected output. \"\"\"Runnable companion for Quantum Programming, Chapter 16.\"\"\"\nimport io, json, sys\nfrom importlib.metadata import version\nfrom qiskit import QuantumCircuit, qpy\nfrom qiskit.primitives import StatevectorSampler\nfrom qiskit.quantum_info import Operator\n\nsecret = \"1011\"  # Written high bit first.\nn = len(secret)\nqc = QuantumCircuit(n+1)\nqc.x(n); qc.h(range(n+1))\nfor qubit, bit in enumerate(reversed(secret)):\n    if bit == \"1\":\n        qc.cx(qubit, n)\nqc.h(range(n))\n# Save/load the coherent circuit before appending measurements.\nbuffer = io.BytesIO()\nqpy.dump(qc, buffer); buffer.seek(0)\nrestored = qpy.load(buffer)[0]\nassert Operator(qc).equiv(Operator(restored))\nqc.measure_all()\ncounts = StatevectorSampler(seed=41).run([qc], shots=1024).result()[0].data.meas.get_counts()\nassert all(bits[1:] == secret for bits in counts)\nrecord = {\"problem\": \"Bernstein-Vazirani\", \"secret\": secret,\n    \"python\": sys.version.split()[0], \"qiskit\": version(\"qiskit\"),\n    \"seed\": 41, \"shots\": 1024, \"qubits\": qc.num_qubits,\n    \"gate_counts\": dict(qc.count_ops()), \"counts\": counts,\n    \"execution\": \"local ideal StatevectorSampler\", \"oracle_queries_per_shot\": 1}\nprint(json.dumps(record, indent=2)) Problem 16.3. What evidence would be needed for a runtime advantage claim? Worked solution A scalable task and access model, matched classical baseline, complete preparation and oracle costs, execution and readout costs, comparable success and precision, and measurements or justified resource analysis over growing sizes. This completes the programming sequence. The companion examples form a locally tested starting point for projects, with hardware submission kept explicit and separately documented. Sources and further study: IBM, QPY serialization . Phase-oracle readout. U_f is the reversible bit oracle on the input register and the minus target, not an elementary gate. The final input measurement reads the encoded pattern."}];
